        <p>emphasised the necessity of forming a strong fund to carry on the campaign. Mr. MAGnzi seconded and said he wished all teetotallers were as moderate as Mr. Roberts, for in his opinion they sometimes defeated their own ends by going to extremes. He disclaimed any hostility to the publican and said the man who conducted his house decently and respectably could always look upon him Nlr. Maginn) as a friend. He also complimented Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons upon the management of their houses, pointing out that no barmaids were employed and that tea and food could be obtained throughout tha day quite as easily as beer. In his opinion Messrs. Walker's houses were the best con- ducted in England. The vote was heartily passed and the meeting closed.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this committee was held at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday, Mr. G. Piling in the chair. There were also present :—Messrs. J. W. Bouchier, W. McDermid, 11. J. Westbrook, T. R. Westbrook, F. Warburton, J. Mynekyme, G. J. Brown, F. Pearson, J. H. (Market W. Mclntyre, G. F. Ashton (clerk), and J. Swinton (surveyor).</p>
