        <p>quite es able to govern their own local affairs as their Rural District Council. Ile thought they had always had been fairly dealt with by the Rural District Council. By sending that report to the Rural District Council the minority were giving back those privileges they enjoyed of governing themselves. Surely it was not to the advantage of the Parochial Committee that a "minority" report should be presented to the Rural District Council and that they should look over their work. lie then expressed his sorrow at the absence of Mr. Hallows as he wanted to tell him that be was making himself a wet nurse for that committee. (Laughter.) Mr. T. R. Westbrook said he did not wish to sly any- thing but to move " That this meeting resents the action of those members of this Committee who sent in the ' minority report to the District Council, with reference to the making of new streets and back passages recommended at the last meotiug, as being uncalled for and not representative of the opinions or wishes of the inhabitants of the parish." Mr. McDermid seconded, and said it was a pity if they could not have a little difference of opinion with- out going to extremes. A good deal of feeling had been manifested in the matter and the committee had reasons for complaint. They had certain powers to act tinder that parish and they devoted their time end con- siderable trouhle to discharge those powers and after they had gone through the ceremonies necessery, it looked as if they would have been much better at home, lie thought it was a reflection on the Parochial Committee to send up that report. There bad been a great deal said that was uncalled for. Mr. R. Westbrook said he could not support the resolution. They did not object to the passages being made and if anybody read the report they would find that they did nothing of the sort. There had been no test of the feeling of the parish. If the inhabitants said they wanted the passages making he would bow to their decision but as it was he could not agree to it. Mr. McDermid was not right when he talked about the Powers being taken from the committee, because the Rural District Council was the head authority. Mr. Brown said there had been no test of the feeling of the parish, and thay hoped they would get it. (Hear, hear.) fie did not know that in the history of the parish eruinority"report had previously been presented. They wanted the hitc'',r_ passages in a fair sanitary state, ttnt at present they were in an insanitary state. He always thought that the majority ruled the meeting and not the minority. It was a very great reflection upon the majority of the committee that that minority report should be sent up. If the parish decided that they were going to be governed by a minority and not by a majority they must find some one else for his place. He supported the proposition. Mr. Bouchier said he signed the report, and he was not at all ashamed of it. He honeyed a parish meeting was to he held, and then he would be prepared to defend his action. Mr. Clarke also contended he was justified in signing the report, and said the 'passages were not insanitary. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried by six votes to three.</p>
