      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>COMPLAINT FROM WALTON.</head>
        <p>COMPLAINT FROM WALTON.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Walton Inferior Parish Council with respect to the depositing of night soil from Stockton Heath on Morley Commons. Complaints had been received about its causing a nuisance. The parish council hoped the Parochial Committee would find another place of deposit. The chairman said the matter was brought before the Runcorn Rural District Council and the inspector was asked to make .a. report on the matter.</p>
        <p>The clerk said the nuisance -1;;;;;;Ctor bad made a thorough examination and he thought there was no ground for complaint. Tne inhabitants had made no complaints at all. The matter was left in the hands of the inspector. TIM STREETS AND PASSAGES. The Chairman said it would be remembered that at the last meeting a resolution was passed for the making of certain private streets and also for the making of certain back passages. Since then a "minority" report had also been forwarded to the District Council oppos- ing the resolution and the matter was referred to the Highways Executive Committee. The Clerk read the report of the Minority Committee which urged the necessity of differentiating between certain streets and passages and submitted recommenda- tions as to the work they thought should be carried out. The making of a large number of streets and passages would add very considerably to the highway mileage of the Rural District and would ultimately mean a substantial addition to the highway rate. They sug- gested that a special committee of the Rural District Council should be appointed to inspect the streets and passages referred to. Tho report was signed by Messrs. J. W. Bouchier, H. Clarke And H. J. Westbrook (with reference to the streets), and by Messrs. Bonchier, Clarke, H. J. Westbrook and J. Hallows (with reference to passages). Mr. Pearson quoted the speeches Mr. H. J. Westbrook and Mr. Bouchier made at the last meeting of the Parochial Committee and said that Stockton Heath was progressing and he thought they would all agree to that. (Hoar, hear.) It would be well for the parish that they should maintain that rate of progress but they could not maintain it unless they made their streets and passages. The signatories of that minority report wanted to prevent that progress, and therefore he wished to express his disapproval of that report. It had always been held by that Committee that they were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington Beat St. Helens.</p>
        <p>Warrington had an easy fixture on Saturday when they were at home to St. Helens. Little interest was shown in the match, and only about 2,000 spectators assembled in beautiful weather. Tho home team had several changes from the side which played in the previous week's cup-tie, and Tilley was still a notable absentee. The teams were as follows: •</p>
        <p>Warrington—E. Jordan back; J. Fish, G. Dickenson, I. Taylor, and E. A'atkinAhree-guarter hacks; J. Lloyd and E. Brooks, half backs; G. Heath, G. Thomas, A. Boardman, P. Boardman, W. Harmer, and W. Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>St. Helens—Smith, back ; Manchester, Hill, Hillen, and M. Creevey, three-quarter backs ; Trewith and Toole, half backs; Briers, Lee, Pope, Mavity, Penning- ton, and Prescott, forwards.... .</p>
        <p>lieferee, Mr. A. Smith, Halifax; touch judges, Messrs. B. Minion, Wigan, and E. Pearson, Leigh. St. Helens had a strong wind behind them in the first half and showed dashing form. Warrington responded with smart combined work but there was a lack of sting about the attack. The Saints got to close quarters as the result of a run by kireevey, and a cross-kick sending the ball over the line, Hillen scored behind the posts,Briers adding a goal. ‘Varrington immediately put more vigour into their play and drove St. Helens back. Fish and Watkin were both stopped dis the line after five runs, but eventually the latter got another chance and beat the defence cleverly. Thomas landed a fine goal against the wind, and the teams were five points each at half time. On resuming St. Helens continued to display smart form, and Manchester came near to increasing their score. Several Warrington efforts went wrong and when at three-quarter time Thomas put the home side ahead with a penalty goal there was a shout of joy. St. Helens had to play second fiddle after this and Thomas made the opening for a try by Belton, Thomas adding a goal. Fish next ran brilliantly down touch and passed inside to Dickenson the latter scoring easily and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>ThOrtlft9 gosling again. In the last five minutes, Dickens,m dlshed through and passed to Fish who romped in ;it the corner. Final : Warrington, 4 goals 4 tries (20 points) ; St. Helens, I goal I try (5 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SfOCKTON HEATH HOUSEERFAKING CASE</head>
        <p>SfOCKTON HEATH HOUSEERFAKING CASE</p>
        <p>Policeman's Narrow Escape.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Wednesday Charles Vincent was charged with breaking and entering the house of Frederick Pearson,Fairholme,Grappenhall- road, Stockton Heath, on March 20, The evidence showed that the house was securely fastened at six o'clock on the night in question. On returning, about two hours later, Mr. Pearson found the front door open. On making an examination upstairs he found several drawers had been opened, and a bunch of keys, a gold watch, and a gold ring were missing. An entrance had been effected by forcing the bottom sash of the pintry window. On the following day Police- constable White, stationed at High Legh, received information of another robbery, and while making inquiries he met the prisoner near Lymm. He took him into custody, and prisoner quietly proceeded with the officer for a short distance. Subsequently he made an attempt to escape. In the struggle prisoner aimed a blow at the officer with a heavy jemmy, which was warded off. The stolen property was found in his poss.ssion.</p>
        <p>r The magistrate (Er. G. Pilling) committed prisoner to the Chester Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SIR J W. CAMPBELL, BART, C.B. Recommends Dr. Cassell's Tablets as .a complete restorative of</head>
        <p>SIR J W. CAMPBELL, BART, C.B. Recommends Dr. Cassell's Tablets as .a complete restorative of</p>
        <p>NERVE ENERGY AND PHYSICAL -VIGOUR.</p>
        <p>- - ---• -- Major General (retd.) Sir John SV. Campbell, Bart, C.R. the well-known hero of the Afghan war as an °lacer of the Royal Artillery, writes from the Primrose Club, 4, Park Place, London, the following appreciative letter in praise of Dr. Cassell's Tablets as a safe and reliable remedy for restoring nerve energy and physical vigour. Sir John Campbell says:—" I have pleasure in stating, that I have derived great benefit from Dr.Cassell's Tablets, and have found them remarkable effective in restoring the nerve energy and physical vigour which advancing years naturally impairs. They suit me admirably and I have every contldenee in recommending them as a safe and reliable bodily restorative." For nervons prostration, antemis, debility, stomach troubles, paralysis of the nerves, heart weakness, kidney troubles, and nearly all forms of nerve and bodily weak- ness, Dr. Cassell's Tablets are unequalled. They only cost 100. (trial size), Is, lcl. and '.2s. 9d. a box of any chemist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ALDERMEN AT LOGGERHEADS. Increases of Salaries Discussed.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) There were also present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alder- man Bolton), Aldermen A. Bennett, T. Burton, W. J. Forshaw' W. Pierpoint, D. Tinnion, Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, J. Ball, T. Barton, J. Broadhurst, T. Chantler, J. Dickenson, James Evans, J. Hawthorn, Dr. Joseph, J. T. Locker, J. Lord, J. J. Nield, J. Pemberton, W. B. Roberts, W. Rogers, J. Sankey, H. 'Singleton, J. Skelland, T. S. Steel, J. Fulton-Smith, F. Stott, 0. Toole, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson, Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), A. T. Holloway (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. I,ongdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas (water engineer), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), F. V. L. Matthias (electrical engineer), J. M. Murray (directnr of education), F. G. Ruddock (borough analyst), and Dr. J. C. Bibbed (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>INCREASE OF POLICE FORCE.</head>
        <p>INCREASE OF POLICE FORCE.</p>
        <p>With regard to the appointment by the Watch Com- mittee of seven additional conatables, the Mayor in reply to Councillors' S ott and Wijson, said the increase was made in accordance with the recommendations of Government Inspector. It had nothing to do with the amount of crime or a reduction of hours, the number being fixed according to the population. It was not the custom to advertise for the men, but he would see that the suggestion of several Councillors that local men should be appointed if possible was conveyed to the Chief Constable. BaDGE-STREET IMPROVEMENT. Alderman BENNETT moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Com- mittee, one of which was to the effect "That the Town Clerk submitted suggested conditions for competitive designs required for premises at Market-gate, and after discussion was instructed to supply a copy to each member of the committee." The Alderman expressed satisfaction at the completion of the widening of Bridge-street. They had had to wait a long time, but the general expressions of satisfaction were some little reward for the trouble and expense which had been incurred. They would be better able to judge of the utility of the improvement when they had further advanced. The committee were carefully considering the terms of the competition with the object of securing that the buildings at Market-gate should be worthy of the centre of the town. The minutes were approved. CONTRACT QUESTIONS. Councillor LORD alluded to a minute of the Health Committee :—" That the tender of Mr. C. Cotton for the erection of a boiler at Aikirt-street Hospital at the sum of £49 10s. be accepted." He said he wished to draw attention to the conditions under which the tendals were asked for. Tenders were advertised but the people who wished to tender had the utmost diffi- culty in getting specifications in time to send the tenders in. Three firms in Warrington had been put to the greatest inconvenience. They sent on several occa- sions for specifications and were repeatedly told they were not ready. They only got them on the Monday morning, or the day before the tenders had to be sent in.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR explained that eleven tenders were sent in, of which four came from Warrington people. Perhaps the complaints pame from those who were dis- satisfied because they did not get the job. If there was any complaint they would do their best to rectify the matter.</p>
        <p>Councillor EVANS said that if a complaint was made it should not have been made to private members but to the Health Committee. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were carried. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee resolved that the tender of Mr. William Winstanley for the painting of the inside and outside of the Baths accord- ing to speoilication, at .£172 Is., be accepted. Alderman BOLTON said the tender was not the lowest but Mr. Winstanley had the scaffolding prepared to carry out the work whilst those whose tenders were lower had not.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said it was very deplorable when the committee had a tender from a respectable man who had served his time with leading decorators with the town and whose tender was £ll lower and they would not accept it. If contracts were to be given in that way there were very poor hopes for the younger generation of Warrington making headway. It was not for the committee to say whether a man had any scaffolding or not. If he took the contract he would have to find scaffolding. Counoillor LORD seconded. He understood there were only about three present at the committee meeting and the contract was decided by a majority of one.</p>
        <p>Ultimately Alderman Bormon withdrew the minute, as the work was not urgent and could be allowed to stand over for another year.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL GIRLS AND THE BATHS. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee resolved that they were unable to make any arrangements for the attendance of school girls at the baths. Councillor STorr moved that the minute be referred rack. and said it was quite as essential thPt the girls should have bathing facilities as that the boys should be accommodated. The attendance at the ladies' baths during the day was not great, and certain hours could be set apart for the school girls. Councillor ROBERTS seconded. Alderman BOLTON said the cause of the Committee's decision was that they were short of accommodation. If the bath were used regularly by the school girls it i would be impossible to keep it n a fit condition for the ladies.</p>
        <p>-After considerable discussion this minute was also withdrawn.</p>
        <p>• INCREASES OF SALARIES. The Gas Committee resolved that the salary of Mr. J. McNichol!, assistant gas engineer, be increased from £l5O to £175 per annum as from the 31st March; also that the salary of Mr. W. S. Haddock, gas engineer, be increased from .£5OO to £6OO per annum. Alderman PIERPOINT moved the adoption of the minutes and asked the Council in common honesty and fairness not to allow the expected :ncrease of the rates to influence them in the matter. Mr. Haddock and his assistant were more than well entitled to the advances, and he loped they would be agreed to unani- mously. Mr. Patterson, who preceded Mr. Haddock, resigned in 1888. His salary was £5OO a year with private practice, which would probably bring the total up to £BOO or £l,OOO a year. In 1888 he was pro- ducing 214,000,000 cubic feet of gas per annum. From 1898 to 1907, under Mr. Haddock's management, the make of gas had increased from 282,000,000 to 441,000,000, land the number of consumers bad increased from 7,919 Ito 14,841. The net profit in 1898 was .C7l lie. 2d. and in 1907 it was £14,700 17s. lid. During the same period the Gas Committee had handed over to the Corporation for relief of rates £62,200. Everyone would agree that these splendid results had been in a great measure due to the careful and clever manage- ment of Mr. Haddock. He had done all this not because he expected more money but through pure business love of doing that which was good and right. At Widnes the gas manager was paid £6OO for his work in cennection with the gas department and £3OO las water engineer: at Rochdale the gas manager's salary was Z5:50 • Salford, £B5O ; Southport, £6OO with house, &amp;c.; Birkenhead, £600; Stockport., MOO. The com- mittee had under consideration the question of giving more gas to slot meter users, and he hoped soon to be able to place proposals before the Council. All he had said about Mr. Haddock applied equally to Mr. McNichol!. Alderman EVANS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lora; -s-aid.h;shad no desire to depreciate Mr. Haddock or his assistant. Alderman Pierpoint had submitted comparisons with other towns, several of which were larger thin Warrington, and he did not give any particulars of the cost of manufacture. lie (Councillor Lord) believed Mr. Haddock was about as deserving a man as they had in the employ of the Cor- poration, but the present time, in view of high rates and bad trade, was not opportune to raise salaries. He moved that the minute be referred back for the present. Councillor BARTON seconded. A long discussion ensued in which several speakers argued that Mr. Haddock's salary should not be raised until the price of gas had been reduced. On the other hand, it was pointed out that it rested with the com- mittee and not with Mr. Haddock to fix the price. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that it was not the Gas Engineer or the Committee who provided the large increase of profits, but the consumers. On a vote being taken the amendment was defeated by 20 votes to 8. Councillor Szorr,who said he agreed with the advance to Mr. McNichol!, then moved another amendment that the increase of Mr. Haddock's salary be referred back. He contended that Mr. Haddock's salary had already been increased proportionately to the increased produc- tion of gas. They had had difficulty in getting justice for the lamplighters and Mr. Haddock was one of the , greatest obstacles to their getting what they considered was only justice. It was all right to keep down expenses but the workers, who were doing even more than Mr. Haddock to produce the proflts,ought not to be crushed down.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor PRNIBEIZTON seconded. Alderman PIERPOINT strongly denied that he or the Committee had any intention of oppressing the work- ingman. If any person would come before the Committee and make a complaint of tyranny—although he (Alderman Pierpoint) did believe there was any— they would give the matter full consideration. The amendment was defeated by 20 votes to 10. The Finance and Estates Committee resolved that the salary of Mr.. Joel Smith, assistant collector, be increas?.d by the sum of £l5 per annum, making a total of 2165.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD moved and Councillor PEMBF:RTOS seconded an amendment that the minute be referred back on the ground that the present time was inoppor- tune for the increase and that Mr. Smith had already reached his masimnin. Couviltor EvA.Ns asked if it was in order to give an increas4 ti) a person whose maximum salary had been reached, without first submitting the question of the maximum to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Best</head>
        <p>Best</p>
        <p>Th e TOWN CLZRK : If you pass this resolution removes the limit. Councillor EVANS : What is the lese of flxincs maximum if by an haphszard resolution of this kind it can be upset? It was pointed out that Mr. Smith reached maximum nine years ago and aeveral Councillors testimony to his long and fa thfnl services. The amendment was defeated, only Councillors Loi Pemberton, Barton and Shetland supporting it.</p>
        <p>SMALL HOLDINGS. LIVELY SCENE BETVVEEN ALDERMEN FORSFIAW AND BOLTON. The minutes of the Small Holdings and Allot Committee contained an item estimating that ti expenditure for the year commencing Aprtl4lst wor be £2OO.</p>
        <p>Alderman FORSHAW moved the adoption of minutes, and said they proposed to grant eight app.( tions for allotments. They hoped to have several in&lt; applications soon. - Councillor WILKINSON seconded. Alderman BOLTON said this committee had ilggun borrowing land from the Town Hall, Parks, and Bat, Committee. rhey had allowed them the use of afield fi temporary purposes, and so that eight men could grc cabbages on the land at a rent of 17s. a year the co milli tr had to levy £2OO out of the rates,or very nearly a farthi in the 2, which they had no right to levy. He did a know why they needed to expend £6O in putting do' a water supply to allow cabbages to be grown, and did not understand why the ratepayers should Sr £2OO simply to put into effect an Act was not understood. The Act said distin there should be no cost to the ratepayers. Th. was no value for money received, it was like payil 30s. in order to give a child a penny cake. There b been no explanation from the Chairman (Alderr Porshaw), and he moved that the item regarding £2OO be sent hick.</p>
        <p>Councillor STEEL seconded, and said he had expect this Act of Parliament would have dons some good Warrihgton, and he wanted to get the report on Act promised from time to time by Alderman Forsbw Alderman FORSICAM : It has not been in exist (in Warrington) more than a month. Councillor STEEL: I want to know what the . Holdings Committee are doing for the benefit of town. We find they have taken a plot of land Victoria Park, and it appears we are going to sr £290, plus £6O for the laying on of water— Alderman FOR.SILIW : No, it includes the £6O. Councillor STEEL: Still it is for the benefit people in or near Knutsford-road. Ishould have thou' for that sum the committee would have done sometb not only for Latchford Ward, but other parts of town. I don't think the provisions are being wit. applied to the town and that is why I second. • Councillor STOTT opposed the amendment, and snit Alderman Bolton had omitted to say the land was bel rented at the rate of £3 an acre. They were not tak the land from the people who used the Park, but ff the tenant of the farm. They proposed to divide land into 24 allotments, and they were confident the would be taken up as soon as the wo4ting men of Latchford Ward or the adjoining locality knew ti were available. If they got 24 applicants at 17s. a 3'4 he did not think the Corporation were going to l( much. He thought the income would more than co' the outlay, and even if it cost a farthing in the port it would be worth while providing allotments. Council had spent money very much more foolishly. Councillor Pamaxaratt : Can we legally put money on the rates?</p>
        <p>ThE; TOWN CLERIC : You are bound to provid,e all 4 ments on application, but there must be no charge the rates.</p>
        <p>Councillor NIEL]) said the item of £2OO was on': provisional item. They might not spend it. If ti spent £2OO the town would have full value of the mo, They had started the scheme on the most practical eacellent lines, and the movement would he belit be attended with the greatest success. Alderman FORSHAW I can quite understand Al&amp; man Bolton opposing anything that is for the wellbeit of the working man. (Cries of "Oh, Oh.") His ex'- to London were £3OO in connection with the chester Ship Canal Bill. Alderman BoTiroN: I object to that. Alderman FORSHAW : And we shall get a return the rates of £2O a year. Alderman BOLTON: You must withdraw. Alderman FORSHAW: I shall do nothing of the He (Alderman Bolton) assured the committee, Rivers Committee, that the whole of the costs (£lo,oon_l in reference to the litigation of the Manchester Shli Canal Company would be returned to the Corporatiop Instead they were only to receive about £2,000. 114 gave them that absolute assurance, and Alder' Bolton ought to send a cheque to the Borough for £7,000. Alderman BOLTON rose to a point of order, and finial It is disgraceful that this sort of conduct should Br wnen he said that I am not the friend of the wort man.</p>
        <p>Alderman ronsmtw: I said you always opposed well-being of the working man. Alderman BOLTON: I don't see why you should this money out of the rates. The MA.YOR : It is quite allowable in debate. Alderman BOLTON: If you want it you shall haVe I am doing as much as anyone else. Councillor STEEL: Will he press the arnendme.- Alderman BOLTON : It is a matter of showing ratepayers that this money is wasted wholesale. .., 401 Alderman FORSHAW : It is not being wasted. - have wasted the money. The amendment was then put, but only Al( Bolton voted for it.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the committee were then agreed to. Shortly afterwards. at the suggestion of the Mal the Council adjourned for ten minutes, the atmosph of the Council Chamber, after a sitting of over t, hours, being very oppressive. When the meml- returned, Alderman BOLTON renewed the discussion with refi</p>
        <p>ence to the Ship Canal Bill, and spoke rather warmly Alderman Forshaw. The MAYOR (indignantly) : We will not have here. Settle it outside if you like. Alderman Bolton,at the end of the meeting,passed the followidg note to the rcporters :—" Total amor" received last year was £3O 25., and not £3OO as by Alderman Forshaw."</p>
        <p>iotio statect</p>
        <p>'THE PROPOSED RE-CONSTITUTION OF TO COUNCIL. Councillor Toole asked if anything had been done bi the committee appointed with reference to the revision of the wards. He did not think they had had meeting; The MA.YOII : I don't think they will have if We nr„ ,to he treated like another townn_ which has beer" revising the wards and every alderman and councill° has to come out and fight an election. Councillor Toomc : That is all the more reason svbl we should go on with it. (Laughter). The minutes were approved.</p>
