      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON STREET ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON STREET ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Damages for Negligent Driving.</p>
        <p>The Worst Corner in Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shines Will, K.C., and a jury, Mary Ann Attwell, 164, NVinwick-street, seed Robert Barlow, coal dealer, Pear Tree Cottage, Knutsford:road, for damages for personal injuries alleged to have been sustained through defendant negligently driving a horse and lurry on November 27th. Mr. F. Cuthbert-Smith (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Browne defended. Mr. Cuthbert Smith said the injury done to the plaintiff, who was 65 years of age, as a result of defendant's negligence was most serious and she could never recover. She was practically deprived of the means of earning a livelihood for herself and for her poor old husband who had for many years been unable to work. She had been trying to keep the home over the heads of the two of them and through the absolute carelessness of the defendant she could no longer do it, but would be a helpless cripple on crutches all her life. The accident occurred at the corner of Horsemarket-street and Bewsey-street. Defendant pulled up his luny outside a cycle shop in Bewsey-street and afterwards crossed from that side, which was his proper side, to the right hand side of Bevrsey-street, so that he could make a bee line across Horsemarket-street into Scotland road. If people liked to take risks of that kind, they must be held responsible for what happened. The lurry was laden with bags of coal, some of which projected over the edge of the lurry. Defendant actually drove into the gutter on the right-hand side of Bewsey-street, and the lurry scraped the kerbstone near the British Work- men's Coffee House. Plaintiff was coming along Horse- market-street towards Central Station, and at Bewsey-street she stood to allow , the lurry to pass. The bags 'which were projecting over the end of the vehicle struck her in the chest with such violence that she fell and also upset another woman who was walking behind her. Plaintiff fell on her back and one of her feet shot over the pavement and the lurry passed over it, with the result that the foot was broken. The one fact which was clear above all others was that the defendant was deliberately on the wrong side of the road. Plaintiff save evidence in support of this statement and said she estimated that her loss of business on laccount of the accident was £l2 10s. Eliza Ho dgett, Russell-street, corroborated. Caroline Rose, 21, West-street, spoke to finding plaintiff lying on the ground and there was no other vehicle but defendant's lurry in sight. Edward Lowe, a newsboy, said that after the accident he ran after the defendant and told him to stop. He saw the lurry go over the footpath. P.C. Baxendale said he came on the scene soon after the accident and took plaintiff to the Infirmary. Defendant said be knew there were two women standing at the corner of the street, but that was all he knew. In answer to Mr. Browne, he admitted that as a rule 999 out of 1,000 drivers crossed the street in the same way that defendant did. Dr. Hodgkinson said that in consequence of the severe crushing the arch of the foot had been destroyed. Plaintiff would probably be a permanent cripple. Defendant was then called. He said he kept in the channel close to Mr. Danks's cycle shop in order to check the speed. After seeing that the road was quite clear he crossed over to Scotland-road. He only took the same course that he had taken hundreds of times before. He did not see anybody on the footpath. Plaintiff had since asked him to give her" few trifles" as compensation but he told her he was not to blame. Questioned as to his means, defendant said a firm of money-lenders had a bill of sale over all he had. James Barlow, brother of the defendant, gave similar evidence. William Henry Bailey, 28, Forshaw-street, said he was standing at the corner of the street and defendant did not drive his horse and lurry any differently from other drivers. There was nothing to prevent plaintiff stepping out of the way and witness had not to step back to avoid the lurry. Inspector Cox, N.S.P.C.C. said he came from the direction of the Central Station and waited for the lurry to pass. Defendant had hold of the horse's head and crossed right over into Scotland-road. Witness saw Mrs. Attwell fall between the two wheels but he could not account for the accident. She seemed to slip. lie did not think defendant could have done anything to prevent' the accident. If he had kept on the left hand side he would have been put to considerable diffi- culty in crossing to Scotland-road. He had had exper- • iPnce in the police force and did not know of a more difficult corner. Mr. Browne said defendant was exceedingly sorry the accident happened but he denied any liability for it. What more could he have done ? There was no footpath on the British Workman side of Bewsey-street. This was to every driver a most dangerous spot and acknow- ledged to be one where care had to be taken. Defendant exercised every reasonable precaution. There was no law as to the right or wrong aide of the road if there was nothing in the way. Defendant's suggestion was that plaintiff slipped and fell. Mr. Cuthbert Smith submitted that there was no defence whatever. There was not a shred of evidence to contradict the story told by the plaintiff's witnesses. If the footpath was not safe for the pedestrian, where was he to go? His Honour having summed up, the jury returned a verdict for the plaintiff with £2O damages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CHEST TROUBLES.</head>
