        <p>‘• Poprilar Weddiog.—Very few weddings have aroused general interest than that of Lord Errington and e't'l Ruby Elliot, whibh took place last Saturday at 4argaret's Church, Westminster, and was attended the Queen, the Dowager Empress of Russia, and a large number of Society people. Lady Ruby V3Peared at the altar in the conventional bridal attire, alt her wedding dress bore a note of simplicity, an t of over-elaboration which was an unmistakable Jlbute to her youth and beauty, and in the absence of her she was given away by the youthful brother Lord,, Afelgund. Many Royal gifts were received by the .'" and bridegroom. Lord and Lady Minto, the Parents of Lady Ruby, have long enjoyed the personal arlendship of the Queen, who presented the bride with d• l'arY special gift, and in addition a magnificent .diarnond pendant lV sent jointly by their Majesties th:l,;:ing and Q teen. Presents were also received from orVrince and Princess of \Vales, the Duke and Duchess q„'ellnaught, Princess Henry of Battenburg, and the "een of Spain. There were a great many gifts too Till India, costly jewels from various native Indian tutees, so that altogether the wedding presents made 11,,,exceptionally magnificent display. The Mcdern Honeymoon.—Singularly enough Lord 1,14 Lady Errington's wedding synchronized with the -6`,",riage of Miss Winifred M. Elliot and Lieutenant elulort Elliott, the bride being the daughaer of Mr. H. _ Elliot of Buen Ayres. The similarity of names was somewhat remarkable, as is also the fact thatd,ieutenant Vici„ M,rs. Elliot are going to spend their holeymoon in TE',6ella, where Lieutenant Elliott has official duties. h e3' are not however the first couple to spend their ta°„ll„,e j°loon in the wilds of Africa, and nowadays newly- -I,oney 1 People chose all sorts of ways of spending the raot'Or rcl°oll in unconventional fashion. Some go on I,n r trips, others indulge in a voyage on a great ocean and 8a and there is a general tendency to prefer the whirl cr,„ "eitement of the city to the seclusion of the e:"PtrY. It is only the Lord Chancellor who has not ..atire freedom of choice in such matters, and when ▪ ore Loreburn was married last autumn, he had to iZegnise the unwritten law that the Lord Chancellor, iu° is supposed to always have the seal in his own ePaing mnst not go beyond the limits of the Kingdom. Easter' Eggs.—The valentine is never heard of w-a-days but the Easter Egg has decidedly crept into • r, and this year the jewellers, florists, and sweet- Cr'ee,taihoonp. Be s ' espeCially aro making quite a display in this Beautiful as the florist's eggs most certainly I,l,ret there is little doubt however that the majority of :a Would prefer those hailing from the sweetmeat ,T.alPorium, or the jewellers. The latter are showing rings, pendants, bracelets, &amp;c., in egg-shaped cases of White velvet, and it would seem as though Easter can n ~ow be seized upon as a season when one may give any- thing, from a packet of fancy pins, to a diamond tiara. an _ been 6 employed Satins • —For some considerable time,taffetas has b as the back ground for transparent, d semi-transparent materials, but the idea just now is, Use satin for the purpose, introducing only one inter- lining, such as chiffon, through which the satin gleams. 1,13.3' modistes, when airy-fairy dresses are under con- , o:toleration, insist upon at least three or four interlinings, but with a satin background, this extravagance can be , dispensed with. Satin is in great demand, not only as back ground for gaudy materials. but for dinner and all gowns, and evening coats. And so beautifully soft are the best varieties that their draping qualities fall very little short of those of cashmere. Skirts and Blouse Matching.—The fancy for making the shirt or blouse, of the same colour as the skirt, a°.lll3' of finer material, grows apace. For example a 'Jule tweed skirt, with a blue voile blouse,trimmed with aerti lace looks well, and a nut-brown coat and skirt, With a nut-brown chiffon shirt, relieved with ivory entbroideries, is to be commended. Contrasts in skirts and blouses are no longer possible, and white blouses and shirts will, as soon as spring advances, be worn only With white skirts. Lighter Colours Prevalent.—There is no doubt that aa a nation the women of our country grow more and More addicted to light hues, which is perhaps just as Well couidering dark colours are found to absorb and live out odours ef all kinds, to a far greater extent than the light, and it is for this reason that professional "rses are not allowed to wear black dresses. It is said that for nurses black cotton is bad, black wool Worse, and black silk the most injurious of all. How to Walk—To walk well and ganef ully,the shoulders Should be thrown back, the spine strightened, and the back just below the waist-line curved inwards. The leg Should swing freely from the hip joint, and the weight of the body should be thrown upon the balls of the feet, uPon the heels. Correct walking means a light, 15: natio, springy step, and a corresponding sense of reedom of action. If one walks correctly one will not get easily tired, and the exercise will be beneficial to the Whole frame, brining the muscles into vigornus 'eau% and aiding the proper circulation of the blood. e"ornen and Drinking Habits.—The Pan-Anglican r,.;tlgress. to be held in London in June, will discuss ' social subjects, one of them being the growing habit of drinking and drug-taking among women. The Inagistrates appear to be pretty well agreed that drink- ing is on the increase amongst women err the working .lasses, and others affirm that it is spreading to the i_oetter, in fact all,classes of society. If that is the case, to will probably be found to be due to no small extent ,a a factor which the Temperance Societies appear to have overlooked, viz., the increased facitities with Nthioh drugs and alcoholic stimulants can now be ,Obtained in convenient form. The latter are consumed in large quantities in the guise of medicinal wines, Which freely and openly find their way into drawin- trooms where the brindy or the whisky bottle would il?Ci looked upon with horror. That, it is to be feared, nas ,become a very common way of cultivating an IPPetite for alcoholic stimulant, and it is a very ,4118,iclious one, because it is not associated with the and ill repute of ordinary drinking. eeping in Church.—Among the relics preserved in "8 of the old churches in London is a bea lie's bwa,ke-up " staff. It is a light rod, having a feather , ' usu. at one end, with a knob at the other, and was ded in olden days to keep people awake during sermon thriaof3 If the sleeper was a woman she was tickled with th, feather brush, but a man had a tap on the head with peo:Tob. No doubt sermons were long and dull, and ratir? were more prone to sleep in those days. At any tot, B'eeiting in church appears to have been such a Itie,;aon practice that many benevolent people con- -ad it their Out) to provide funds fr the sped-</p>
        <p>I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Examiuer, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiuer, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE SECRET OF EXTRAVAGANCE.</p>
        <p>Credit, declares Mrs. Hugh Adams, is the real secret of modern extravagance. Give a woman credit, and you throw open the gates of Eldo- rado. There is practically no limit to her ex- penditure. With credit. behind her the sanest woman loses her head and shops recklessly, be- traying that inherent penchant for extravagance i which s the birthright of her sex. Make that same woman pay solid cash for what she buys, and you may trust to her instinct that she gets full value for every penny she spends. Her love of a bargain comes keenly to the fore. Count- ing coins has the magic value of making them go twice as far. But credit alters •her whole point of view. The fact is. a woman cannot think in figures. Her sense of proportion gets muddled. In nee eyes credit excuses the most flagrant extrava ganoe. It blunts the saner, steadier senses of prudence and restraint, just as it blunts the sense of taste while it sharpens and develops a desire for novelties that are merely costly and bizarre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SIMPLE AND PRETTY NIGHTGOWN.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE AND PRETTY NIGHTGOWN.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our sketch is a simple but charm- ingly pretty nightgown, which is designed in the latest and most approved style. The original came from a famous French lingerie house, and formed part .of the trousseau of a smart young bride who was married a shore time air°. A small square yoke forms the upper part of the "nightie." this yoke being made of nainsook edged at the top with a small scallop, and</p>
        <p>lightly embroidered with a simple design of flowers and spots. Round the top of the yoke come a series of buttonholed slots, through which is threaded a rather narrow ribbon, which is tied in a knot in front. The bottom of the yoke is finished with a line of fine feather- stitching. All the fulness of the garment is set on to this yoke in a succession of small pin- tucks, . the fastening being arranged in the middle of a narrow centre box-pleat. Simple bishop sleeves, taken at the wrist into a plain embroidered cuff shaped like a man's shirt cuff, complete this pretty model.</p>
        <p>91112....4v . Pr4-t0,,) . 1Z ...• ..--1 ..Ff-:'•-•;:' . I 0141 1,1 1 i $5 I I 1 ill I ' I 0 ,` s ' • h •, k , I! t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MILLINERY MODES.</head>
        <p>MILLINERY MODES.</p>
        <p>As in dress, so in millinery, the brightest of eoloiirs predominate this season, declares Lon- don Opinion. Hats are both extravagant in mode and expensive to buy. Large picture hats in bright-coloured straw, lined with a paler or contrasting shade of straw and trimmed with a profusion of feathers or a wide band of roses mounted without foliage and finished off with bright quills of unusual length, are now taking the place of the once popular satin cloche hat. Equally popular are small " beehive " or " pud- ding-basin ' hats, made with high crowns ani narrow brims that turn up in front. These are trimmed with swathings of satin and bright quills and wings. While other fashionable bats boast of crowns covered with masses of bright- . coloured foliage and adorned with huge up- standing Paradise plumes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FANCIES IN SEALING-WAX.</head>
        <p>FANCIES IN SEALING-WAX.</p>
        <p>- In Indian lao work, melted sealing-wax in a great range of colours is twisted -round and round sticks or balls from top to bottom in al- ternate bands. Each article is then held before the fire, and with a needle or pin short lines are hero and there drawn perpendicularly througb the bands of sealing-wax, drawing the different colours into each other, when the stick or ball is rapidly rolled on a cool, smooth surface, and that intricately variegated effect is produced which , is so puzzling until explained. The netted mats are made by allowing the thread of sealing-wax twisted round a stick to cool and then drawing off the whole coil and breaking it into sections of three or four turns each, which are linked together into "mats" of all sorts of variegated colours, but chiefly scarlet and black, and black and golden yellow. This Oriental pro- cess, described by Dr. Birchwood, C. 5.1., may, the Queen thinks, suggest some new devices' to modest home-workers, who have for the nonce I adopted as a hobby the decoration with sealing- wax of hatpins and jewel-boxes.</p>
        <p>A NEW</p>
        <p>PLEATED SKIRT.</p>
        <p>In spite of innumerable predictions to the contrary, the pleated walking skirt, or " trot- tkur," as our French neighbours call it, is more fashionable than ever, and will be muoh in evi-</p>
        <p>dence durm' g the spring and summer. • Our sketch pictures one of the. newest French pleated skirts, a smart, becoming, and most practical model. As you will observe, the skirt is very wide, all the fulness being taken into a suc- cession of deep tucks at the top, which are so neatly and accurately fitted as to form a sort of sheath round the figure. Tis skirt, needless to say, is cut of useful walking length.</p>
