      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>KIDNEYS SAIITES.—Choose two large kidneys, and cut them the round way into thin slices. Have ready a tablespoonful of flour, well sea. I soned with pepper and salt, and dip each piece of kidney in it. Cut some thin, neat squares of streaky bacon, fry them very slowly in a little butter. When done, put them in the dish for serving, and keep hot while you sauté the kid- neys, which put into the fat the bacon was cooked in. Take care the kidneys cook gently, for if done fast they will be hard and indiges- tible. In about a minute the gravy will begin to rise on the upper side, then turn the kidneys and let them finish cooking slowly. They will no none us lour or live minutes. fut them on the dish with the bacon, and pour over them a spoonful or two of beef gravy, or water thick- ened with a little flour, boiled and mixed with the fat and gravy from the kidneys in the fry- ing-pan. &amp;rye in a hot-water dish.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PIE RATES.</head>
        <p>PIE RATES.</p>
        <p>BUDGET NIGHT AT THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>An Effort to Reduce the Estimates.</p>
        <p>Tuesday was Budget night at the Warrington Town Council meeting and Alderman Bennett, as Chairman of the Finance Committee, submitted a Budget involving an increase in the rates of 10d. in the £. He said : My task to-night, is not a pleasant one. Twelve months ago," Hope told a flattering tale." To-day, Experience will attempt to point a moral. The blunt fact is that whilst a year ago, the rates were only seven shillings in the £ to-day they are is. 10d., and, like the Cornishmen in Old Trelawney's days, our seventy thousand citizens would "know the reason why." I wish it to be clearly understood that THE RESPONSIBILITY BELONGS TO EVERY MEMBER OF</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL, as well as to the Finance Committee, and I want you all to clearly understand it. I wish to make my explana- tion so complete that everyone in Warrington will understand it too. Luckily, the shock of the disclosure has been somewhat discounted by the announcement which appeared on Saturday in the local papers. And the explanation is comparatively simple. We will take the borough rate first. A year ago we owed the bank £1,120. To-day there is a balance due to the bank of 1E1,490, or an increase of £370. This is owing to the fact that every department, except the Gymnasium and the Watch Departments (to which I give due credit), has exeeded its estimates. The increase in the overdraft would have been larger still if it had not been for the fact that the receipts, in several instances, reached a higher figure than was anticipated. The estimates for the ensuing year, in almost every instance, also show substantial increases. They are as under : Watch Committee, £292 ; General Purposes, £9O ; Cemetery, £B7; Parks, £37; Baths, £183; Gymnasium, £4 ; Education, £2,658 ; Small Holdings, £2OO ; making a total of £3,351. The Town Hall Com- mittee shows a decrease of £Bl ; rivers is the same as before (£200) • and finance has fallen by £568 ; which leaves a total increase of £2,952. The largest items are Watch Committee, £292, mainly due for provision for several extra constables; Baths, £lB3, for estimated cost of painting, extra wages, AG. ; Small Holdings, £2OO, and Education, £2,658, a figure which is mainly due to increased salaries, and provision for medical inspection, feeding of necessitous children, whilst the sinking fund and interest on the new schools must necessarily be an increasing amount. The great offender, is, of course, the , EDUCATION COMMITTEE, which itself accounts for close on threepence in the £. In 1904-5, the amount required from the Borough Fund for educational purposes was £10,408 ; in 1905-6, £11,843 ; in 1906-7, £14,733; in 1907-8, £16672; and in 1908-9, £19,330. In five years, the amount has nearly doubled, the total increase amounting to about nine- pence in the £, whilst the total sum required by the Education Committee amounts to nearly one shilling and seven pence. Deduct that from the 7s. 10d., and our rates would be only 6s. 3d. in the £, even in a heavy year like this. And I fear that we are not yet at the end of the journey. New schools will certainly be needed. More onerous conditions will, from time to time, be probably imposed on the authorities. Against this increase in the estimates on borough rate account we have, luckily, an even greater increase in the estimated receipts. Last year, they were estimated at £10,700 ; this year they are estimated at £14,337; a difference of £3,637. But I am bound to point out to you that this is due to an entirely exceptional circum- stance. It will be remembered that we have spent, in recent years, something like £lO,OOO upon</p>
        <p>THE LONG AND TEDIOUS LITIGATION with the Ship Canal Company. Luckily, the end has justified the means, and we have been successful all along the line. And it is computed that we shall receive at least £3,000 back towards the costs within the next financial year. I hope that we may get a larger sum. But that much is practically certain. The borough rate, of course, includes the poor rate, and the overseers estimate that, in order to provide the necessary working balance to meet the needs of the Poor Law Guardians, they will want to make a first call of £8,124, a second call of £9,400, and that their other expenses will amount to £6OO, making a total of £lB,lBO. Last year they only called upon us for £15,000, and the difference of £3,180 plus the amount necessary to make up the working balance which was considerably reduced last year necessitated an extra rate of 4d. in the L. To come to the improvement rate. The total over expenditure is not less than £2,931. The DEPARTMENTS WHICH HAVE KEPT WITHIN TITEIII ESTIM ATES are the Longford. Intercepting, Scavenging and Street Improvement Departments, and I wish to give them all a friendly pat upon the back. These facts, together with the further and still more important fact that the Gas Committee, last year, only handed us £ll,OOO, retain- ing their surplus profits, as a provision against renewals which may soon be needed, has resulted in the startling phenomenon that, instead of having a balance at the bank upon the improvement rate account of £2,783, as we had a year ago, we have an overdraft of £2,518, a difference to be provided for of £5,304. The estimates have also gone up under nearly (weep head : Nightsoil Department, £317 ; Longford, £341 ; health, £l4B ; hospital, £549; highway, £270 ; fire engine, £3 ; markets, £32 ' • weights and measures, £lOO ' • street improvement, £329; finance, £231; making £2,320. The Intercepting Departments show a decreased estimate of £l3 ; and the Scavenging Department of £l3O, reducing the amount to £2,177. Receipts, however, are estimated to produce an additional £329, and this reduces the amount still further, and leaves a net increase upon the improvement rate account of £1,848, so that we have to provide for increased estimates, £1,848, to replace the balance at the bank last year £2,780, and to pay off the present overdraft of £2,517,a total of not less than £7,151. This would mean a rate of over 7d. in the £, but happily, the Gas Committee have once more COME TO 01111 RESCUE, .</p>
        <p>by promising to provide £1,500 over and above the £lO,OOO which they originally promised, coupled with the extra productiveness of the improvement rate, will enable us to meet our own obligations by an increase of 6d. in the Z. Next I must says word about tbe general sewers rate. The amount required this year is £2,449, as against £1,207 last year. The result of all these figures is that the improvement rate will be increased from 2s. 61. to 35., the borough rate from 4s. 4d. to 4s. Bd., whilst the sewers rate will remain the same 2d., making a total of 7s. 10d. in the £, or an increase of tenpence. The position is, therefore, roughly this. If all the committees had kept within their estimates and if the Gas Committee had been able to hand over the same surplus as in 1906-7, the rate, last year, of seven shillings in the would have been adequate, and we should not have been called upon to meet the over- drafts we have to face to-day. But not only have we to make good the loss occasioned by these causes, but we have not any balance in hand towards the present rate. And these two factors dominate the situation. I wish to point out, as emphatically as I can, that whalitsver rates we levy, be they high or low, their adequacy will depend, in the main, upon the committees. It is impossible to make ends meet if they persistently exceed their estimates. Upon one point, at least, we may congratulate, ourselves. I doubt if there is a town in England where the rates are collected as well as they are collected in Warrington. When we recollect that the poor rate amounted, last year to over £52,000 and that the arrears were only £l6 2s. 6d that the improve- ment and sewer rates amounted to nearly £30,000, and that the arrears were only £5l, we can form some small idea as to the ability and tact and energy of our Collector, Mr. W. H. Woodcock, who has again been very highly complimented by the district auditor. I can very cordially extend the compliment to the Borough Treasurer and to the whole of their respective staffs. (Applause.) And I should like to remind you all again that, from another point of view THE TOWN'S POSITION IS EXTREMELY STRONG, for, whilst our liabilities, last year amounted to £1,273,337, our assets reached the enormous sum of £1,729,830, a surplus of not less than £456,543. I pointed out, twelve months ago, that much has been already done to bring Warrington into line with the best and most progressive county boroughs in the kingdom. We have bought our gas and water works ; re-built the one and greatly extended the other; estab• listed our electricity station and our splendid tramway services ; built our police court and technical school ; much enlarged our market ; erected fine new baths ; acquired Victoria Park, and widened Bridge-street, not tO mention scores of minor improvements. The widen- ing of Bridge-stre'et is, at last, complete, and as I have already said, everybody in the town is proud of the result. But it has cost over £130,000, and is still, in my opinion, twenty feet too narrow. And Buttermarket, Sankey-street, and Horsemarket are all awaiting our attention, nay, indeed, are clamorously demanding it. And there is a bridge across the Mersey to be built or widened—built, I hope—and a new bridge at Howley to provide, and the Baths to enlarge again, and the Town Hall td extend, and the nine bowling greens to he provided, Ad a host of other things which claim attention. We cannot shirk, or even indefinitely delay, improvements so much needed by the town. But we want to count the cost, to husband our resources And to "look before we leap." The point which specially forces itself upon my attention is this:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>IS WARRINGTON PROGRESSING</head>
        <p>IS WARRINGTON PROGRESSING</p>
        <p>with sufficient rapidity to guarantee these improve- ments? Is our ratable value increasing iO6 proportion to the growth of our expenditure? I find that the total expenditure, in 1901. when we had a 7s. 100. rate before, 'Pass £83,045 : 1905, £83.324; 1906, £78,172 ; 1907, £81,782 ; 1908, £92,983 ; making £421,306 These violent fluctuations ought to be avoided. They make a basis bf comparison extremely difficult. That the increase in the town's expenditure should not outstrip the growth of its rateable value. This, I find, has increased in the same period from £251,203 to £279,226. a total increase of £28,023 or an average of close upon £6,000 per year. I may add that, in 1901, the mean pro- duce of a penny rate, was about £9OO. it is now pro- ducing about £l,OOO. This in tolerably satisfactory, so far as it goes, but the town is not increasing with any</p>
        <p>great rapidity, and, in spite of all the splendid oppor- tunities it has to offer, there is a curious dearth of new industries. We want to attract these. They will not only find employment for a bigger population, but increase our assessments and correspondingly reduce the rates. Outside the normal growth of the assessments, where are we to look for further revenue ? It never yet did hurt To lay down likelihoods, and forms of hope, And I am, personally, very sanguine as to the future of our great reproductive undertakings. The Gas Works has done splendidly, thanks largely to Mr. Haddock and his staff, and will probably, ere long, do better still. The Electricity Department made a gross profit, last year of £7.709, and, after proving for Sinking Fund and interest, of not less than £2,517; this was carried to Reserve P and, which already stands at £6,952. As soon as it reaches a tenth of the Capital Expenditure, a time which is rapidly approaching, it will, I trust, be able to make substantial contributions in relief of the rates. The Tramways made, last year, the sum of £7,093, and, after providing for Sinking Fund and Interest, were able to transfer tke sum of £1,310 to Reserve, which stands at £1,530. They, too, in time, will doubtless come to our relief. And, in spite of the enormous cost of the extensions, the Water Works are getting within a measurable distance of making both ends meet, and will, no doubt, eventually produce a profit which can be applied to the relief of the rates. Moreover, I believe that, in the early future. we shall see great and TJNEEPECTED DEVELOPMENTS OF MUNICIPAL ENTERPRIZE, and that these, in turn, will pour their treasures into the coffers of the Corporation. The Town Planning Bill already points in this direction,and the Corporation may become large property owners, I trust at a fair profit. The borough, too, will gradually extend its boundaries. We must not weep, like Alexander, because we have not any other worlds to conquer. What about 'Stockton Heath? [Alderman Bolton: We don't want them. Laughter.l New sources of municipal taxation, too,may be devised,such as the taxation of unused building sites, for instance. And one may learn from Mr. Burns's Bill that there is some reason to hope that the periods of repayment for loans on work of a permanently useful 'character may be extended. It is not fair to saddle one generation with the total cost of such improvements as Bridge-street, for example. The Old-Age Pension Scheme if carried, as I hope it may be, will also tend, I trust, to much reduce the Poor Rate, and there are other possible sources of relief which will readily occur to all of you, and on which I must not dwell. I should like to have said something with reference to the proposal to appoint a professional borough auditor, to which I have given considerable attention, and which I regard as of the first importance. The position under the existing law is anomalous, if not absurd, and although not less than three audiors are now appointed. we have not any guarantee that wren one of them shall be a properly qualified accountant., That I regard as essential, by whatever method we arrive at it, and on several grounds/pre surely should not rest until the law is altered. Pending this I think we ought to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A CHOIR TRIP.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A CHOIR TRIP.</p>
        <p>£25 Damages for a Crushed Finger.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, TLC., and a jury, Albert Fairhurst (16), suing by his father, Thomas Henry Fair- hurst, farmer, Appleton, claimed damages for personal injuries from the London and North Western Railway Company. Mr. F. Cuthbert Smith, barrister, (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. F. L. Lambert, solicitor, London, represented the corn-</p>
        <p>pany. Mr. Cuthbert Smith said the Company were sued for injuries sustained by plaintiff owing to the negligent act of a servant of the Company in slamming the door of a railway carriage in such a way as to trap one of the fingers of plaintiff's right hand. The defendants had exercised a very wise and proper discretion in not con- testing the qusation of whether their servant was negli- gent or not. They admitted he was negligent. Therefore the only questicin for the jury was as to the amount of the damages. Plaintiff was a member of the Church choir at Stretton and in July last they had a picnic to Blackpool. One of the church- wardens, Mr. Willett. had arranged with a clerk for a special saloon to be attached to the 5.30 a.m. train from Bank Quay. When the train came in it was fonnd that there was na saloon, and the party naturally. con- cluded that that was not their train. When they had waited about for some time there was a cry of "Take your seats" and they were eventually bundled into the train. Some half-dozen boys, including the plaintiff, got into one carriage. Fairhurst was the last to get in just as the train moved off. He had his right hand upon the jamb of the door while he tried to pull the door to with his left hand, when a porter abut the door and the tip of his right finger was trapped. One of, the boys opened the door and released the finger which bled very profusely. Plaintid fainted away and was unconscious for some time. There was no one to take him home so he went to Blackpool where instead of having the happy day which was advertised on the posters, he Spent the time first in having the finger attended to and afterwards proceeding to a chemist's shop where it was dressed. For the return journey the saloon was provided. Next day plaintiff was seen by Dr. Bower who found that the top of the finger nail Was completely off, the bone was lacerated and there was also bruising. The finger was now healed. The doctor's bill was five guineas, .£3 12s. was charged for extra food and nourishment, and the boy was for some weeks unable to help his father on the farm. He was also entitled to compen- sation for pain and suffering. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out this statement. He admitted that his father only • paid him Gd. per week for helping him on the farm and that whilst suffering from the injury he had the same kind of food as usual. Dr. Bower stated that his bill was five guineas which was a reasonable amount. Mr. Lambert submitted that in view of the slight injury sustained, plaintiff was only entitled to small damages. The doctor's and chemist's bills should be allowed but no other actual expenditure had been proved. The jury awarded plaintiff £25 damages together with the doctor's bill, Is. chemist's charge and Is. 6d. choir money lost by the plaintiff, making a total of .230 7s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Parochial Mission Women Pond, Bank Quay.</head>
        <p>Parochial Mission Women Pond, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>The following is the report of the Lady Superinten- dent (the Hon. Elisabeth Wilson-Patten) for the year ending 30th November, 1907 : —" The work of the Mission has been carried on as usual during the past year. The Mission woman (Mrs. Hunt), paid 13,875 visits during the year and ,the average attendance of women at the weekly meetings was 61. The deposits amounted to £5.6 7s. 41d. When the books were closed for the year the members numbers 360. There have been many retnovals from the neighbourhood during the past year, which fact accounts for the average attendance and the numbers generally being slightly less than previously. Lady Frederick Cavendish visited the Mission on October 26th, when 130 members were present. The annual picnic, which the members pay for themselves, took place on July 29th, Southport being the place selected. Most of the ladies connected with the Mission went with the party The tea party, given by my sister and myself, took. place on the 18th January, in Holy Trinity schoolroom 230 members were invited, and after tee the Rev. H. A. Lester delivered an address."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>For Supper.</head>
        <p>For Supper.</p>
        <p>Brown Sz Poison's "Patent" Corn Flour prepared with good milk as a hot soup or slmple baked pudding makes a light supper dish, such as imposes no ta,. upon the digestion, and induces a good night's rest. • A few sultanas added makes a nice variety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>APPOINT A PROFESSIONAL AUDITOR</head>
        <p>APPOINT A PROFESSIONAL AUDITOR</p>
        <p>on our own account. We have the power under one of our local Acts. And now I must conclude. My faith in Warrington is one of the main passions of my life. I have dreamed of it as a great and beautiful and prosperous city, and ever since I began to take an interest in its municipal affairs at all. But as George Meredith once wittily observed of one of his heroines, she had to " plump her exquisite proportions on bread and butter." (Laughter.) And the fairest super- structure ever reared mast rest on brad and durable foundations. And the best foundation for the city which we seek to build is, after all, judicious business management and a sound financial system. (Applause). Alderman BENNETT then moved resolutions fixing the rates as follows :</p>
        <p>S. d. Poor Rate Improvement Rate General Sewer Rate 0 2 7 10</p>
        <p>Councillor Wrr.triNsos: seconded. Councillor PEMBERTON said 10s. was such an alarm- ing increase that it would bs better if the estimates were referred back to the committees with a view to reduction. He was confident that several committees of which he was a member would be able to reduce their estimates. He did not think there had ever in the history of Warrington been an advance of 10d. in the .0 and when the estimates were drawn up the committees did not anticipate such an increase. He suggested that the Council should adjourn for a fortnight. The TOWN CLLRE: : It will have to be some day this week. The MAYOR : We have no money to go on with. Councillor PEMBERTON : Why, you have had a bank overdraft of £130,000. Councillor STEst...said he understood the Council had no power with regard to the poor rate. The suggestion was that the Committees should consider their estimates with a view to reducing them. He supported that because the proposed increase was so alarming. Councillor SINGLETON said he understood there was an increase in the poor rate of 4d. He had made inquiries from Htlion officials and they said it was not so. After further discussion it was decided on the pro- position of Councillor PENIBERTON, seconded by Alder- man BOLTON, to adjourn the Council meeting for a week, with the object of each co nmittee revising its estimates with a view to effecting some saving, the , whole estimates to be afterwards submitted to a meet- ing of the General Purposes Commitee to be held prior to the Council meeting..</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COIIPOUND ' for Coughs and Colds Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
