      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTOM UNION APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTOM UNION APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>Selected Candidates for the Offices of Clerk and Assistant Clerk. The Warrington Board of Guardians met in com- mittee on Wednesday afternoon to open the applica- tions received for the offices of clerk and assistant clerk rendered vacant by the tesignation of Mr. J. C. Sutton and Mr. S. Sutton. For the clerkship there were 60 applications and for the assistant clerkship 102. The following candidates were selected to appear before the Board on Wednesday next :</p>
        <p>FOR TIIE CLERKSHIP. Arthur Bottomley (33), chief assistant clerk Salford Union, eight years. Previously chief assistant clerk Bramley Union, four years. Thomas Henry Bentham (26), assistant clerk Preston Union, from February Ist, 1904. Previously second assistant clerk, Ormskirk Union, seven years. Cornelius Cole (39), clerk to the Newton Urban District Council since August 1900. Previously nine years deputy clerk to Hereford Guardians. After- wards was for eight years chief assistant clerk to West Bromwich Guardians. Leonard Wray Greenhalgh (30) deputy clerk Chorlton Union, since September 1903, with 13 years experience, five of which as deputy clerk -, has six assistants acting under him. He is responsible for all accounts, conducts part of correspondence and when clerk absent undertakes all duties. William Henry Roberts (30), assistant clerk to Holy. well Union,and Rural Council. Son of clerk to ditto. Eight years as his assistant and four and a half years assistant clerk to the Union. FOR THE ASSISTANT CLERKsurp. George Harker (23),clerk in office of Drifaeld Union and Rural District Council, since May, 1905 ; was clerk from November 1902 to May 1905 in Union work, North Allerton.</p>
        <p>Robert William Peacock (35), in the service of the Clerk of this Union, nearly four years, was for six years senior clerk in the Melton Union offices. Has had 16 years experience of Poor Law work in Unions. Henry Parton (25), private clerk to clerk in Burnley Union nine years. We understand that before applications for either post were considered age limits of 45 for the clerkship and 35 for the assistant clerkship were fixed and this fact is likely to give rise to discussion next Wednes- day when the final appointments will be made.</p>
        <p>CONTINUOUS NEGLECT OF THE EYES is the most fruitful source of serious eye trouble. If you cannot read the small print in the newspaper ONE EYE AT A TIME 12 inches sway not further,JOHNSTON WATSON, EYESIGHT SPECIALIST, should be con- suited at once.--81, Bridge St., Warrington. Low Charges. Satisfaction Guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
