      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>APNIL lltb, 1908.</p>
        <p>, Nobody on either side of THE NEW the House of Commons has PREMIER. the slightest doubt that in his new position as Prime Minister Mr. ASQUITH will make a splendid display of Parliamentary ability. His great talents are unquestioned, and, indeed, beyond question. He has been a most exceptionally successful man. 'THICKERAY has told us in one of his "Round , about Papers" how Milo kurdav was seen to be ' marked out for greatness almost from his child- hood. At school, at college, among the rising men ' of letters, in the society in which he mingled, it ' seemed to be agreed by common consent that he Was to be a great man. So it was with Mr. Asotunt from at least a little later age. •Be carried everything before him at Baltic'', U JOWETT'S day, when he had for coin panions Or Competitors many men who have since risen to eminence. He no sooner appeared in a law case than he became singled out as one to whom it would be judicious to send further briefs. In the Parnell Inquiry he was not , even overshadowed by RossuLn, and he became a • conspicuous figure in the Liberal Party, from the Moment in which he spoke, many years ago now, on the platform of the National Liberal Federation It at Nottingham. - Some South African tribe conferred on Mr. 'CHAMBERLAIN a title signifying that he was the Imo who got things through—an unhappy choice of designation, seeing how little Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has got through, and how much of what he has done could have been left alone with great public advantage. In the case of Mr. ASQUITH such a , titular description would have had the merit of being accurate. He really does get things through. Yet, despite the general acknovvledgment of his Uncommon abilities, there is obviously a doubt in some quarters as to whether he possesses quite those qualities which go to the making of a • successful PRIME MINISTER. What the indispens- , able qualities are nobody is able to say. In Lord PALMERSTON they were certainly not the same as in Mr. GLADSTONE. Could there be a trio of more sharply contrasted characters than Lord DERBY, 4INJAMIN DISRAELI, and the MARQUESS or Silas- -33"Y F Yet all these ware facile leaders of men, and highly-popular figures with Parliameot and the nation. Mr. GLADSTONE had something more—a great deal more, in fact—than any Of them. He could send a thrill through the heart of the country as none of the others could. He had personal magnetism and the power of inspiration. What qualities does Mr. Asournt lack? It may be just these, perhaps. The light of his intellect is supposed to be cold and dry. "There is no thrill in him," say some of his critics. But this is just what we have yet to dis• cover. The new Prime Minister is one of those Men who never seem for a moment in doubt. His judgment is rapid, like a flash. He is asked a question and he raps out a sharp reply. Probably he is not in the least thinking of his manner ; but other people are, especially his questioner. He gives the impression of extreme hardness. When he replied to a question concerning the Irish political prisoners, Mr. JUSTIN MCCARTFIY, a Sensitive man with an imagination, said that he closed the prison doors with a clang. He had nothing whatever to do with the shooting down of the miners at the Featherstone Colliery yet there are those who continue to believe that he had because they are convinced by the set of his features that he might be capable of giving very stern orders indeed. But what is probable is tA that the nation at present does not more than half know its ASQUITH. What a man will do as Prime Minister cannot, in fact, be known—c ninot even be intelligently surmised—in advance. Take the Me of Sir 1113NRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN, Even men like Mr. WALTER LONG professed to make merry with the idea that it was possible for him to become First Minister of the Crown. What is the opinion now ? Brief as has been his tenure of power, he has made his • position as one of the great historical Premiers There is much about Mr. ASQUITH which we do not know and which we are now to learn. The Me may be the same with himself, for men are seldom much better informed about themselves than they are about their neighbour. The hard manner may disappear now that there is less</p>
        <p>freedom and more responsibility. A ripened and mellowed Mr. AsQuail would be an ideal Prime Minister and political leader.</p>
        <p>The announcement made on Monday morning that Sir Henr y Campbell- Bannerman had tendered his resignation to the King, and that his Majesty had sent for Mr. Asquith, could scarcely be said to take the public by surprise. We have all hoped against hone (says the " Westminister Gazette ") at hat Sir Henry would be restored to health and enabled again to take up the burden of his high office; but Hit is an imperative necessity that he should seek relief from these responsibilities, we must accept his decision, with whatever reluct- ance. Courage and character shin° out in his public record, end nothing, we may be sure, has beaten him which mortal strength could resist. Let us hope that the , repose which he will now enjoy will lengthen his days and bring him the peace which the veteran has earned. In the past ten years he has passed through ordeals as fierce as ever fell to the lot of fighting politician, and has met great and sudden changes of fortune with the same equal mind. The qualities which he has shown in this period are •quals which the English people prize in their statesmen, and it has been made abundantly clear</p>
        <p>" C. 8."</p>
        <p>. .</p>
        <p>to us all in the last few weeks that he has won his place in the hearts of his countrymen. We will not dwell at this moment on his services to his party or the irreparable loss which his party suffers by his withdrawal ;it is sufficient for us to-day that there should on all hands be gegerous ackuoiv- lodgment of his great services to his country.</p>
        <p>Last night's demonstration in For the Bill! favour of the Licensing Bill may ba described as historic, for the reason that it concentrated local temperance sen- timent and conviction into one broad channel. I The phases of the tight over this Bill have done more than anything in our time to mark a distinct cleavage between the public and the brewer, and Parr Hall was filled to overflowing with an audience conspicious for its earnestness and deter- ruination both individually and collectively. Tee brewers have blustered and threatened too shame- lessly and too long! The people have arisen, and the auguries for a revolt in Warrington against the oppressive yoke of the Trade are unmistakeable. Men like the Bishop of Liverpool have given a lead which commands a whole-hearted, single- minded following and we have faith that the army which has volunteered will march unchecked to a triumphant victory over the demon of drink.</p>
        <p>-- That the Licensing Bill is A REBUFF FOR rapidly gaining popular THE BREWERS. favour becomes increasingly evident etty by day. At first the Trade had all its own way and influenced public opinion by vehement clamour, gross misrepresentation, and cheap appeals to sentiment. But now, the people are sitting down to think for themselves with the natural result that they see through the hollowness of the brewers' pretence to care for " the liberty of the subject," " true temperance " and the like. Only last week in , Warrington the audience at the Conservative open-air meeting negatived the resolution against the Licensing Bill by a three to one vote. But there is even more significant news from Crewe. On Monday evening a huge meeting was convened in Crewe Town Hall by the Licensed Victuallets' Association. Sir Gilbert Greenall presided,and was supported by Lady Greenall, Mr. J. J. Whitley, and many others with more than a casual interest in the trade. The speakers were •accorded a fair hearing, though there were many interjections which indicated the temper of the audience, and at the end Sir Gilbert put the usual resolutions about confiscation, etc. The first was lost by a large majority,and after endeavouring to avoid admitting the fact Sir Gilbert was compelled to say " there were more against it than for it." The second resolution, which stated that the Bill would throw hundreds of thousands of honest, industrious men and women out of employment shared ' an even worse fate. A correspondent who was present at the meeting writes us to say that " the motions against the Bill were overwhelmingly defeated, only about 50 supporting them out of a gathering of 1,500 to 2,000 people" This is a nasty smack for the Squire of Walton and the other poor millionaires with large brewery interests. If they cannot get support for the "Confiscation and robbery " cry at a meeting called by the Licensed Victuallers they might as well abandon the attempt to spoof the public.</p>
        <p>Both the expected and the THE RATE 3. un-expected happened at Tuesday evening's Council meeting. As we were able to 'foretell last week, Alderman Bennett introduced a Budget involving an increase in the rates of 101 in the but the Council adopted the unusual course of adjourning for a week and referring back the estimates to the Committees with a view to their effecting some economies. In this they showed a wise considera- tion for the ratepayers. Tenpence is an alarming increase to come into force at one stroke, and the news created consternation. It behoves all the committees to see that the strictest economy is exorcised; and there are hopes that next week it will be found that the demand is capable of some reduction which, however small, will be thankfully welcomed. It will no doubt be inevit- able that certain improvements will have to be I sacrificed or postponed and probably such schemes as the baths ext :nsion, and the provision of four new bowling greens will run the risk of being hung up for some time. We are strongly of opinion I that preference should be given to the baths enlargement for it is admittedly an urgent necessity, whereas bowling greens —desirable as they certainly are—can hardly be regarded in that I light. Alderman Bennett is to be c.unplimented I upon the lucid and interesting speech in which he introduced what is generally regarded es a " dry" topic. It was couched in such admirable terms that the Council, despite a long and tedious sitting and the unpleasant news which the Alderman was unfoldingffound it not in the least dull and listened attentively. The cause of the present financial 'position is not far to seek. Last year there was general overspending by the committees, and their estimates for the coming year are consider- ably increased ; the amount available from gas profits is £2,500 less, and the overseers require a large increase in order to enable them to meet the calls of the Guardians. It would appear that of late the Council has been cutting its cloth too fine or else that the Committees have shown a fl tgrant disregard of the estimates. It is useless to levy a rate which will barely meet the requirements and then proceed to spend money with a lavish hand. Caution must be the order of the day and much as we all desire progress and improvements-, the rates must be kept within reasonable limits if Warrington is to hold out any strong inducement to manufacturers to avail themselves of its natural advantages and thus increase the rateable value.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Volunteers 1 FAREWELL TO THE are "no more," bat the VOLUNTEERs. territorial force will Anon ' be in full activity in War- rington as in other towns. It is somewhat difficult to realize that we shall no longer hear of the Ist Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment which has been so long and honourably associated with Warrington. Instead, the auxiliary forces— "terriers " they have now been christened in some quarters—of Warrington and district will hence- forth be known as the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment,- the object apparently being to associate them more directly with the line battalions. This is indeed a critical time in the history of the movement, for the operation of Mr. Haldane's great scheme means a new system of voluntary defence. The ex-Volunteers who desire to continue to serve their country as members of the territorial force have to re-attest, but for all practical purposes the conditions of service are identical with the old ones. True the men will be liable to be called upon with the reserve in time of national peril, but there is abso- lutely no compulsion to serve abroad. The dawn of the territorial day was fittingly marked at Warrington on Saturday by a parade of the battalion, and judging by the enthu- siasm manifested the old town will maintain the i worthy traditions of the past in the military ' world. There was just a tinge of sadness about the proceedings,for Colonel Edelsten,the respected commanding officer, bade farewell to the battalion in order to enter upon the important duties of Secretary of the West Lancashire Division. He has worked heart and soul for the success of the ' 'V olunteers and we are confident he will have the beat wishes of those who have served. under his command and indeed of all his fellow townsmen ' in his new sphere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
