      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RATES.</head>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>A THREEPENNY REDUCTION OF DEMAND POSSIBLE. We understand that the Corporation Committees i have met during the week for the purpose of recon- sidering the estimates with a view to reducing them. Some slight reduction has been made and it is possible that by a revision of the estimates of the Education Committee and the grants from the Electricity and Tramways Department, a reduction of the demand to the extent of 3d. in the I—making a rate of is. 7d— may be effected.</p>
        <p>For Chronic Chest Complaints, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</p>
        <p>Warrington Burning Fatalities. Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, conducted two inquiries at the Warrington Police Station yesterday morning on the bodies of two young people who had met their deaths by burns. The evidence in the case of Mary Fenton (21), a domestic servant, in the employ of Mr. Arthur Taylor, butcher, of Buttermarket-street, was to the effect that on Monday last she was engaged in washing operations, when, in taking some water out of the boiler, her clothing caught fire. Her injuries were of such a serious character that she died a few hours after admittance into the Infirmary. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death." —The second inquiry was relative to the death of Agnes Fox (10), daughter of Samuel Fox, of Neston-street, who died on Tuesday. It was stated that the mother of the girl had gone across the street to inform a relative of another death. She had only been gone a few minutes when she heard her daughter screaming, and found her with her clothes on fire. In reaching an article from the mantelpiece her clothing became ignited. A verdict of " Accidedtal death" was returned. Premier in natue, Premier in quality. PREMIER CYCLES from 6 guineas.—J. S. GARLAND 51 to 55 Bridge-street Warrington.—Advt. P.S.A Meetings. The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) presided over the meeting of this Society on Sunday, and Mr. Tuckman, of Manchester, gave an address upon " Courage." As the spine was the great support of the body, so courage was the great support of character. Many men did great deeds of daring for their country or for others in time of danger, who showed no courage when it was a question of morals or character. Mr. W. Booth sang "My hope is in the everlasting" and , " Sun of my soul,' which were greatly appreciated, and Mr. Payton presided at the organ.—There was a a large attendance of members and friends at the open 1 meeting of the Buckley-street Society on Sunday,when an address by Mr. Hindley, of Bolton, was listened to with great interest. Mrs. Simpson occupied the chair. Mr. W. Tynan's rich tenor voice was heard to distinct advantage in two solos, and Miss Woodland and Miss I Spruce pleased their hearers with their rendering of two duets. The afternoon's lesson was read by Miss L. Fairhurst, and Miss M. Plinston was the accom- pianist. —The usual weekly meeting of the Warrington ' P.S.A. Sociesy was held in the Co-operative Hall on I Sunday last, when there was a very good attendance. I The meeting was presided over by Mr. Pierce Williams, who spoke on the Licensing Bill, showing I the many benefits that would arise to the general good of the country if the same became law. —A very impressive address was also delivered by Councillor Ball on " The Personality of Christ, and its relation to Individualism." Solos were very ably rendered by Miss Hamer and Miss Ada Hamer; prayer was offered. by Mr. Heywood, and the lesson was read by the President.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NEW COUNTY JUSTICES. LOCAL APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>NEW COUNTY JUSTICES. LOCAL APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>We have it on unimpeachable authority that a well- Town Councillor presented a bogus ticket at Parr Hall, but failed to pass the judge. * * * The rates are not "up 10d. " yet. The Council was so alarmed at the position of affairs on Tuesday that the matter was adjourned for a week, and instructions given to Committees to do without cream in their tea —otherwise cut down estimates. * Short as it has been, Sir Henry Campbell Banner- man's tenure of office as Prime Minister has been longer than the second Premiership of Pitt from 1804 till his death in 1800, than the Premierships of Canning or of Goderich and of Lord Rosebery, and than the first Premierships of Lord kI elbourne, Sir Robert Peel, Lord Beaconsfield, and Lord Salisbury, and the third and fourth Premierships of Mr. Gladstone.</p>
        <p>* * * There was a certain amount of uneasiness among the members of the Town Council on Tuesday, which was due to fleas. It seems that the Museum has been presented with" fleas from Bank Vole, Bowden "by a Mr. Coward, and Councillor Ashcroft voiced the feel- ing of many when he said " there are enough fleas in Warrington." Mr. Coward's fleas are certified to be dead. For safety's sake it is to be hoped that the Museum authorities will keep them gummed down or locked up.</p>
        <p>* * * There is still hope for the school-girls who desire to have the use of tbe public baths. The Committee was compelled to withdraw the resolution "that they were unable to make any arrangements for the attend- ance of school-girls," so the mattsr will have to be re-considered. We hardly see how the Committee is to solve the problem unless the accommodation is largely increased, but there is no doubt as to the importance and urgency of the question. Public bathing facilities are essential to pnblic health. * * * The "forged ticket " trick did not work at Parr Hall last night, and those misguided servants of the Trade who anticipated having a gay and riotous time inside were nicely foiled. The publicans and sinners came up to the door as bold as brass with tickets which would not pass the scrutineers, and were gently but firmly guided to the rear by the Chief Constable and his trusty men. It was screamingly funny at times to see well-known men who own allegiance to the Trade trying to pass through with a little bluff. The reverse at the door was certain and afforded huge fun to the crowd of onlookers who had got over a similar rebuff and stood by to enjoy the discomfiture of others. The resignation of the position of Prime Minister and First Lord of the Treasury by Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman involves, of course, a relinquish- ment of the right to attend Cabinet meetings unless the retiring Premier be specially invited by the Sovereign, acting upon the advice of the new Prime Minister, to retain his seat in the Cabinet. When Lord Sidmouth resigned the Seals of the Dome Department at the close of a long official career, in which he had at one time been Prime Minister, he retained his seat in the Cabinet at the express command of the King and by the earnest request of all his celleagues. * * * Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman is one of a small group of members who commenced their Parliamentary careers in the year 1868,others being Mr. J. Gr. Talbot, Mr. Chaplin, and Sir C. Dilke, but of these only the first-named, in addition to Sir Henry Campbell-Banner- man, has sat continuously throughout the intervening period. Sir Henry is the present Father of the House but if that position were attributed to the member whose original election dated back the farthest it would appertain to Sir F. S. Powell, who was first elected as long ago as 1857.*</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith has just accomplished the ambition which, according to Mr. H. W. Lucy, he has held, and even expressed, from the beginning of his career. Mr, Lucy, writing in 1898, quotes an anonymous states- man to this effect : —" He (Mr. Asquith) is not a man who wears his heart upon his sleeve, nor is he given to vain boasting. Yet eight years ago, whilst he could not be said as yet to have made his mark upon the House of Commons, I heard him, at a friend's dinner- table, quietly announce that he intended some day to be Prime Minister. The third party to the conversa- tion was Lord Randolph Churchill, who afterwards agreed with me that the aspiration, bold as it seemed at that time,was by no means improbable of fulfilment." * * * Notwithstanding the assured large increase in the rates which is coming, three Corporation officials were granted advances of salary on Tuesday evening. We are glad of it. Take the case of Mr. W. S.Haddock, the Gas Engineer, whose advance from £5OO to £6OO was the only one involving a substantial sum. Why should Mr. Haddock be penalised because the rates are higher? He has rendered splendid and profitable service in his responsible position for 20 years. Under his management the Gas Department has handed over £73,000 to the relief of the rates in eleven years Surely the town can afford to give him another £lOO a year when he deserves it. The advance will have no effect on the rates, and to deny it to him and the other officials, would have been carrying the spirit of economy —with which we have full sympathy—to unwise and unfair lengths* * * It is curious to note just now that in Morley's "Life of Gladstone" the name of Mr. Asquith occurs only once, and, that is in a foot-note consisting of a list of the Cabinet of 1892. It might have been supposed that some mention would have been inevitable in the account 9f the Parnell Commission, which occupies a whole chapter. In this section of his book, however, Mr. Morley performs a remarkable feat. Although no essential fact is omitted, it would be impossible to discover from this source the names of the Irish ex-M.P. who brought the libel action against the "Times," of the three judges who presided over the Commission, or any of the counsel, of the manager of the " Times " who told the story of the purchase of the letters, of the purveyor of the letters, or of the Fenian spy. So rigidly does Mr. Morley confine him- self to. his purpose of writing a biography of gr. Gladstone and not a history of the period.</p>
        <p>What is there that in stress or storm Will rouse the blood and keep one warm? We answer—" Try in either form, Life Tonic." From IL M, ASHTON, 61, Sankey-et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>,„</p>
        <p>Musical Success. At the Leigh Eisteddfod held on Saturday last, Master Charles Eckersley, pupil of Mr. Charles Henry Dawson of 59, Sankey-street was successful in gaining a prize for pianoforte playing. COAGULINE," RUN K,"TEN ASITINE.'--Cements oft broken and other articles.—Advt. Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall. An exceptionally attractive entertainment will be presented at the Parr Hall for a fortnight,commencing on Monday next, by the London Animated Picture Company. The programme will include pictures of the Ober Ammergau Play together with numerous other coloured and stereoscopic living pictures which are all stated to be new to Warrington. It is claimed that the company's pictures attain the highest standard in animated photography, being perfectly steady and not flickering in the least. Bible Class Concert. A successful concert promoted by the members of the Golborne-street Young People's Bible Class, was held the other evening, the Rev. R. E. Letheren, the newly-elected pastor, in the chair. Solos were rendered by Miss L. Plinston, Miss Tanner, and Mr. W. Tynan, duets by Misses K. and A. Hamer and Misses R. and M. Tynan, quartettes by Mr. Barnes and party, recitations by Miss C. Lawton, and cornet solos by Mr. Rutter, the whole entertainment concluding with a sketch given by four members of the class I entitled " Wanted, an actor." The proceeds were in aid of the Church funds. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SIIIRE..—Advt. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Batter market Street.—Advt Death of Mr. Fred W. held. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death which took place a few days ago ef Mr. Fred W. Neild a son of the late Alderman William Neild who was Mayor of Warrington in 1808-9. The remains of the deceased gentleman, who was born in December, 1851, were interred in Broomfield Churchyard, Chelmsford. Mr. F. W. Neild was for many years in the service of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles at Hallam Forge. Rugby Works Competition. The following are this week's results of the first round of the Rugby Works Competition: On Monday evening the points were allowed to go to Greenalls on account of Naylors not being able to raise a team. Tuesday, Firth Company 8, Orford Tannery 0. Wed- nesday, Perrins Tube Company 15, Castle Rubber 2, Thursday, Pearson and Knowles (Dallam) 5, Corpora- tion of Warrington 3. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. Fire in Winwick-street. A fire broke out in the shop of Messrs. Wynne and Company, milliners, Ac., Winwick-street, in the early hours of Sunday. It was discovered by a pedestrian, who at once gave the alarm at the fire station and Superintendent Turner and the Deputy-Superintendent quiekly turned out with 20 men -only one short of the full strength of the brigade-nod the steam fire - engine Captain. The fire, which broke out in the lower part of the premises, was confined to the front shop and the flames were extinguished in a quarter of an hour. The damage is comparatively small. The cause of the outbreak is unknown. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. Good Friday Football. The Rev. H. B. Firth, writing in "St. James' Parish Magazine " says :—" To me it seems an impossibility that any earnest Churchman or Church- woman should lend any aid to the desecration of Good Friday. 'ls it nothing to you all ye that pass by ? ' The football matches held on Good Friday always seems to me to be an indication of the utter forgetf nines on the part of a laige section of the nation, of the first principles of the Christian religion. I hope that you, at least, will show by your refusal to share in the unseemly amusement of Good Friday and by your presence in church, that the solemn com- memoration of your Saviour's death is a very sacred day and one on which you cannot be lighthearted." ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt Farewell Social. The Branch room of the Independent Labour Party was filled at a social held during the week for the purpose of bidding "goodbye " to two of the mem- bers—Mr. and Mrs. Edward Lees—who are removing to Coventry. Councillor Ball made a presentation to Mr. Lees on behalf of the members assembled, and paid a high compliment to the recipient for his active work on behalf of the party daring his five years residence in Warrington. Mrs. Stott made a presenta- tion to Mrs. Lees on behalf of the women members of the Branch. Songs were rendered during the evening by Misses Leicester and Heywood, and Messrs. Nield, Birchall, Pemberton, Murby, and Leytham. A duet was given by Mr. and Miss White, and a part song by the I.L.P. choir. Mr. W. Nightingale gave a humour- ous recital. Miss Leicester was an efficient accom- panist. Councillor Stott presided. SIX GUINEAS will purchase a fully guaranteed PREMIER CYCLE. Inspection invited.—J. S. GAR- AND, 51,53, &amp; 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Late Miss Watson. A memorial service to the late Miss Agnes Watson was held at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening. The Rev. John Yonge preached an impressive sermon from the text" Fallen asleep in Christ" (1 Cor.xv.,pt. 18). The great loss which had fallen upon the family and friends of Miss Watson appealed with singular pathos and force to all the Wycliffe community. The young people of Wycliffe had, many of them, enjoyed her friendship and companionship both privately and in the fellow- ship of their Y.P.S. She had a sunny disposition, a cheery look, a buoyant demeanour, and she enjoyed living. The members of her class in the Sunday school spoke of her with great appreciation as having been ,so much their friend and so usefully their teacher. Mr. Yonge extended the deep sympathy of the congregation with Mr. Watson in his great bereavement. The choir rendered the anthem "Abide with me." MOUNTAIN FLAX (LINTIM CATHARITCUM) PILLS An agreeable aperient. Worth a trial. OP.—Advt. Anti-Vivisection Society. The annual meeting in connection with this Society was held at the Old Academy on Tuesday, under the presidency of Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins.—A resolution of sympathy was passed with Mr. Robert Pierpoint on the death i of his sister, Miss Pierpoint, who was a vice- president of the Society.—Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes submitted the report, which stated that though pro- gress was slow, and the list of subscribers seemed small, they had the consolation of knowing that they had many people in sympathy with them, and they hoped in time that they would be able to join the Society.—Mr. Holmes presented the balance sheet, which showed that the expenses amounted to £l3 10s. 4d. There was a balance in hand of £3 ss. 9d.—Mr. O'Callaghan, of London, then delivered an address on the question of vivisection, and was cordially thanked on the proposition of the Rev. C. Harvey Cook. PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Adyt. " Canada bv Cinematograph.'? This was the subject of a highly interesting lecture delivered in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening by the Rev. James Boyle, Secretary of the Colonial and Continental Church Society, before a fairly large audience. The Rev. G. B. Thurston, Rector of Lymm, presided, and announced that he himself hoped to take up work in Canada at the end of this year. He also mentioned that the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton's son was doing a good work in Saskatchewan. The lecture was illustrated with a splendid series of cinemato- graph views, and convincing evidence was afforded of the commendable work which the Society is doing amongst our kinsmen who emigrate to Canada. Ur. Boyle declared thot Canada offered the soundest possible investment to the Church of England, £1 spent on Christian work there yielding better results than anywhere else.—A vote of thanks was accorded ' to the lecturer, on the proposition of the Rev. B. Percival, seconded by the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth. Success of the Warrington Male Voice Choir. The Warrington Male Voice Choir, conducted by Mr. W. S. Nesbitt. were successful in winning the first prize in the Male Voice Competition at the Leigh Eisteddfod on Saturday last. Their rendering of the test piece " Spartan Heroes " (Protheroe), greatly pleased the adjudicator, Prof. Caradog Roberts, M.B. On Thursday the members held their annual general meeting and dinner at the Winmarleigh Cafe, when 50 to 00 members sat down to do justice to the Cafe Company's well-known catering. The choir, which is self-supportitig, was never stronger than at present, either in members or funds, and with their talented conductor at their head they may look forward to even greater progress in the present year than in the past. During the evening a presentation was made to Mr. S. Has'sall, in recognition of his long and valuable ser- vices as conductor. Owing to business pressure he has had to relinquish office, but he is not severing his connection with the choir. The presentation took the form of a large framed photo of the choir and framed illuminated address. For Children's Hacking Cough at night, Woods Great Peppermint Cure, Is, lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
