      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS. The Runcorn companies of the sth (Earl of Chester's) Cheshire Regiment of the Territorial Army paraded to the Parish Church on Sunday morning, in command of Major Ashton and Major Timmins, There was a large turnout. The members and officials of the Urban , District Council, including the chairman (Mr. Thomas Jones), walked in front of the band. After the service a long-service medal was pinned on the breast of Drill Instructor Colour- Sergeant Plant. WIDNES PARLIAMENTARY BIL.L MOTOR-BUS SIIRVICE ALLOWED. The Widnes Corporation Bill was considered on Tuesday by the Police and Sanitary Regulations Com- mittee of the House of Commons over which Mr. Corrie Grant presided. There were originally several petitions against the bill, but the points to which they were directed have all been settled. There was, consequently, no opposition to the preamble, which was formally proved by Mr. Oppenheim,the town-clerk,and the committee proceeded to the detailed consideration of the clauses. GAS AND W.A.TER. The clauses relating to the gas and water undertaking of the Corporation were first dealt with. Mr. Wedder- burn, K.C., who with Mr. Jeeves appeared for the promoters, explained that the original bill contained a clause empowering the Corporation to apply a portion of the profits of the gas undertaking to the relief of rates. The ratepayers' meeting, however, rejected the , clause. They were afraid apparently that it might ' effect the price of gas, which,he mentioned incidentally. ' was the lowestrin the country, being Is. 3d. per 1,000 feet to the ordinary consumers, is. Id. to large con- sumers, and 11d, for power. As to the water under- taking, the Local Government Board had reported that two townships within the Widnes area of supply,Cuerdly and Bold,were receiving water from other corporations. In the meantime, as an immoderate supply was neces- sary, Widnes had agreed to allow St. Helens Corpora- tion to give that supply temporarily. But they were , perfectly ready to supply water on statutory terms. , They had an abundant supply, and in addition they had . a call upon Liverpool. There was, therefore, no reason Ito interfere with their powers. They had not made 1 default in any way. 1 Mr. Carr, the gas and water engineer, having given ' evidence, the committee passed the clauses. 1 PROPOSAL TO REGULATE HAWKERS REJECTED. Mr. Wedderburn explained that clause 21 which empowered the Corporation to issue market permits for ' the sale of goods elsewhere than in the market on pay- ment of certain tolls, was the same as that which had I already been granted to St. Helens. Mr. Clark, Chairman of the Markets Committee, stated that the Corporation were able without difficulty 'to regulate their own traders. But they were subject to incursions of hawkers from Liverpool, St. Helens, Warrington and Runcorn, who, especially when there was a glut in the market, brought goods to Widnes, raced through the streets first thing in the morning,and sold them without paying anything in the way of tolls or rates. The Chairman : Do your traders ever go into other towns, and do the same thing ?—I don't think so. I Ithink other towns have already the regulations which we now seek. The Chairman: There is, I believe, no precedent for this proposal. Mr. Wadderburn : We have the precedent of St. Helens. It is true it was established so long ago as 1869, but some things improve with age. Mr. Boyce (of the Local Government Board) : This Forty- new justices were on Thursday placed on th power has been refused for the last fifteen or twenty Commission of the Peace for the County of Lancaster years. The local gentlemen appointed are : The Chairman: The clause must be struck out. James Douglas Buckton, V.D., Bruche Hall, War- MOTOR BUS SERVICE. rington,retired lieutenant colonel, Auxiliary Forces. M. Wadderburn next dealt with the clauses empowering the Corporation to provide an omnibus Charles Edward Parker, Penketh Lodge, Penketh, eservic