      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift, for the defence, stated that the room was intended only for stewards, and plaintiff had no right to go into it. The magistrates said that they considered there had been a technical offence, but it was of such a paltry nature that they would not convict, but ordered defendant to pay £1 is. court costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CANDIDATE FOR NEWTON. At a private meeting of the executive council of the Newton Division Conservative Association, of which Lord Newton is president, the question of a Conserva- tive candidate to succeed the late Colonel Richard Pilkington, ex-member for the division, was discussed. It was decided to invite one of two of the late colonel's sons, Mr. Lionel E. Pilkington or Mr. C. It. Pilkington, whichever will accept, to succeed their father as the next candidate.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF DR. PATTERSON, OF EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>------- - On Monday afternoon, the interment of Dr. J. N. Patterson surgeon-major to the let V.B. (Prince of Wales) South Lancashire Regiment. took place at Earlestown. The deceased gentleman was given a military funeral, the South Lancashires attending with band in strong force. There was also a detachment of county constabulary, Dr. Patterson having for a great many years been their surgeon. The Newton-in-Maker- field Urban District Council (of which he was a member since the establishment of the councils), the Warring- ton Board of Guardians (of which he was a past chairman), the Loyal Viaduct Lodge of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity (to which he was medical man), the Lodge of Faith, 481, Ashton-in-Makerfleld ; Makerfield Lodge, Newton-le-Willows ; Chapter, Royal Arch, and Mark Lodges of the West Lancashire Province of Free- maions, and the general public were also largely represented.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL. The monthly meeting of the Newton Urban Council was held in the Council Chamber at the Earlestovvn Town Hall on Monday evening. Mr. T. Ormand pre- sided and the other members present were :—Messrs W. Thompson, W. Collingwood, L. E. Pilkington, A Tunstall, M. Seton, G. F. Clarke,J. Navlor, E. T. Evans, C. B. F. Barron, E. L. Bent, and C. Cole (clerk), A. Bowes (surveyor), R. T. Surteses (water engineer), and Dr. Watkins (medical officer of health). SMALL HOLDINGS AND ALLOTMENTS ACT. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on March 23rd, a circular letter was read from the Clerk of the County Council with respect to the Smail Hold- ings and Allotments Act, 1907. It was resolved that by way of ascertaining whether there was a demand for Small Holdings or Allotments in the district, the letter be published in the minutes. REPAIR OV ROADS. At a meeting of the Highways Committee a letter was read from the County Council enquiring whether this Council were willing to accept a sum not exceed- ing £650 for the cost of maintenance, repair and improvement of the main roads in this district for a period of one year from the Ist April next, and a sum not exceeding £ll5 towards the cost of flagging up to 6ft. wide of the footpath on the north side of St. Helen,-road, from Market-street to Swan-road. It was resolved that such offers be accepted. The minutes were approved. MEDICAL OFFICER RE-APPOINTED. At a meeting of the Sanitary Committee on March 23rd a letter (dated the 13th inst.) was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning the re-appoint- ment of Mr. H. E. Watkins as Medical O'ficer of Health for the district from the 26th March inst., to the 26th March, 1909, inclusive, with a salary at the rate of £llO per annum for his services. The minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Kendars'iNisit.</p>
        <p>The first visit of Mr. and Mrs. Kendal to Warring- ton has this week been in tile quite an important dramatic feature. There is no better-know!' or more popular couple now on the stage, and wherever Mr. and Mrs. Kendal appear, crowds flock to witness the high-class performances which they and their talented company submit. Unfortunately, Warrington has only been favoured with three nights of the Kendals, but those nights have been made die most of by the ' discerning public. There was a very large and repre- sentative audience on Monday evening when Mr. and Mts. Kendal opened with" The Elder Miss Blossom." This is probably the best and most charming piece in their repertoire. The comedy is so refined, and so artistically blended with a touch of pathos, as to rank high in stage productions of this class. It gives peculiar and special scope to the natural abilities et Mr. and Mrs. Kendal, and there is nothing in which we like- them better. The story —by Ernest flendrie and Metcalfe Wood, who both act in the play—is refreshingly simple, Andrew Quick, F.R.S. sends a proposal of marriage fa) the elder Miss Blossom in mistake for her niece, on the eve of his departure for three years' exploration in foreign countries. The proposal is accepted by letter, and during the whole period of his absence the rniddleaged explorer conjures up visions of the prospective young and beautiful wife waiting for him in England. The elder Miss Blossom also con- jures up visions. Joy has code into her life —late may be—but still it has come end under its influence her nature expends. The play opens just prior to Andrew Quick's return. He is eagerly awaited, but chance lias it that he shall first meet with Miss Sophia Blossom, the younger, and the lady whom he imagines he is engaged to. The misunderstanding causes some delightful blundering, which is pour- trayed with a humour positively irresistable. In the next scene, the now-enlightened Quick has to break the news to Miss Blossom. She awaits him with a lover's joy ; he is consumed by mental anguish. For a few minutes the contretemps is funny, but it quickly changes to deep pathos as the truth is borne to the brain and heart of Miss Blossom. The scene is one of tine power and shows the Kendals at their best, though the chief honours undoubtedly go to Mrs. Kendal, whose restrained, vet emotional acting was perfection In the last act the tangle is straightened out. Mr. Quick finds that his love for the younger Miss Blossom has been supplanted by a real admiration and affection for the elder Miss Blossom so that all ends happily. Miss Jessica Thorne won warm praise for her graceful amid pleasing interpreta- tion of the part of Sophia Blossom. She was natural throughout. As Christopher Blossom, Mr. Hendrie was a huge success. His heal ty laugh was sufficient in itself to secure the approbatioli of all hearers, and his bearing generally bespoke the country gentleman in every line. One of the most difficult parts in the 'comedy is that of Withers, Mr. Quick's nervous, stammering, cadaverous valet, and at the same time it is, in the hands of Mr. Metcalfe Wood, the funniest thing seen on the Warrington stage for a long time. Withers had only tb walk across the stage for the audience to roar with laughter. In a company so wholly strong and resourceful it is difficult to discriminate further. Mr. Bassett Roe added much to the humour of things, as Major Twentyman, and Mr. T. Holding made a capital golfing clergyman and lover. Mrs. A. B. Topping won many a laugh as" Cooper," and Mr. R. Burnett also distinguished himself as Tyrel Jones, of the " Hambledon Guardian." On Tuesday evening, the Company produced " Still Waters Run Deep," and on Wednesday " The Iron- master," large and enthusiastic audiences assembling on each occasion. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. • "THE PRODIGAL SON."</p>
        <p>Our readers will be pleased to hear that Mr. George D. Day's Company is engaged to appear next week at the Court Theatre in " The Prodigal Son," by Hall Caine. The resemblance to the story of " The Prodigal Son " as told by St. Luke, is striking ; but in Mr. Hall Caine's story it is the elder son who is reduced to poverty and it is the younger son who comes home rich. There is also a variation in the ending of the novel and the ending of the play. The drama is one that appeals alike to the cultured and the uncultured, for the reason that it deals almost entirely with the simplest passions and temptations of frail humanity. Mr. Day's Company includes many well-known names, the principal parts being filled by artistes of the highest reputation. Miss Winifred Ingham plays the dual parts of Thora and Elia in which she has achieved splendid succe9s. The play is elaborately mounted, the scenery being an exact duplicate of that used at Drury-lane. We have no doubt that the enterprise of the management will be rewarded by crowded houses and seats should be booked early.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 91d. &amp; 1/li. Of Cbemists.—Advt.</p>
