      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Colonel Ildelsten's Farewell to the</head>
        <p>Colonel Ildelsten's Farewell to the</p>
        <p>Battalion.</p>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>My dear Beautiful Warrington Children, I am sure you are all beginning to love Mother Earth, now that your eyes are opened to the lovely gilts which she presents to us every year. I think you are learning to value their beauty more, for I hear that flowers are growing in window-boxes in Sankey-street and Bold- street, and none have been plucked, although they are I within your reach. I was glad to hear that, as I feel certain more people will brighten our streets with pretty window-boxes now that they can trust you not to touch their flowers. I have seen lovely flowers growing on the window-sills of the houses in the Lake District and no one dreams of taking one. In Switzer- land I saw trees laden with apricots tk n d grapes growing by the road-side, and very tempting the fruit looked, 1 but the Swiss boys and girls gaze, admire, and pass on, without yielding to the temptation to pluck any. How nice it will be when all the children of Warrington pro- tect beautiful growing things instead of destroying them. Boys and girls, do your best to prevent those who do not belong to our Society from taking Iloweis out of the gardens and window-boxes in our town and then the owners will have greater pleasure in planting flowers.</p>
        <p>Soon the Easter holidays will be here and many of you will be flocking to the Park. How often have you feasted your eyes upon the lovely flowers there and then sadly gone home, wishing you had a garden, no matter how small where you could grow a few flowers to tend and love. Bat there are tiny " gardens" which belong to most families in Warrington and how sadly neglected they are ! The six feet of soil in" God's Acre " is generally covered with weeds and grass. Very lovely flowers are bought week by week to place upon the grave but ro attempt is made to grow flowers on it. I want you children to remedy this sad state of things. Your parents are probably too buoy ; but you will have plenty of time during the holidays and I am certain they will consent to your sowing some seeds in these tiny " gardens." April is the month for gardening and sowing seeds. Deeply dig the soil so that it will break up finely. .:‘lanure it well, or sprinkle it with a penny packet of " fertilizer " and the April showers will mix all together. Plant Virginia stock round the border and canary creeper near the headstone. The sexton will tall you what other plants are likely to grow. And now how are you to brighten your homes ? Get some small boxes from the grocer and fill them with road sweepings. Boy a penny packet of nasturtiums and sow them in the boxes. Place the boxes on the window sills and you will have pretty flowers of your own grow- ing to admire all the suanner.—Yours affectionately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>VOTES Foil WOMEN PASSED. The Women in Full Force.</p>
        <p>Our local Parliament brought the session to a close on Thursday evening with an innovation, ladies being allowed to address the House in support of Women's Suffrage. The attendance of members was below the average, but the public assembled in strong force, the fair sex being very largely represented. The ladies had by far the best of the argument, in fact hardly anyone had the temerity to oppose them seriously. Perhaps this was in a great measure due to the terms of the resolution, which did not go the "whole hog," but only committed the House to the granting of the vote to woman on equal terms with man. This was carried by an overwhelming majority. THE LATE DUKE OF DEVONSHIRE. At the outset the CLERK read a letter from the Duchess of Devonshire expressing her sincere thanks for the kind message of sympathy and appreciation sent by the Society. SIR HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN. Mr. J. F. AWBERY (secretary for Ireland) in the absence of the Premier, moved a resolution of sympathy with Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman on his enforced retirement.- All sections of the House would, he believed, agree that bir Henry had admirably followed in the line of Premiers of this country. (Cheers). Counoillor NIELD (leader of the Opposition) seconded and said the one outstanding feature of Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman was the sincerity which characterised all his political actions. What he spoke came from his.heart and he did everything with the earnest conviction that it was in the interests of the country. (Cheers). The resolution was unanimously passed. VOTES FOR WOMEN. MA. EDMUND OSBORNE (Newcastle-on• Tyne in a maiden speech opened the debate by moving :—" That the time is now ripe for the granting of the Parliamen- tary suffrage to women on the same terms as it is or may be granted to men." He began by commending the means which the suffragists had adopted to bring their views before that House. They might have been tempted," he said, " to unfurl their banner or throw their arms round the Speaker's neck—(laughter)—tilt the wig of the Clerk of the House, or even to pinch the Secretary.( Laughter.) But from all such devices to attract attention they have refrained.", Had the same hearty sympathy been extended to women elsewhere as by that Society, continued Mr. Osborne, there would probably not have been the same unfortunate breaking of the law and its accompanying penalty. The women were asking that the same liberty and justice which men demanded for themselves should be extended to them. They did not ask for any special privileges and benefits. What they.did ask for was equal rights,that they should not be disqualified by marriage or sex alone from voting at Parliamentary elections, if otherwise qualified, that they might have equality of opportunity to fulfil the onerous duties of citizenship. They believed that could only be brought about by women's suffrage—the vesti- bule of their emancipation. Women needed freedom to be truly womanly as much as man needed freedom to be truly manly. (Cheers.) Mr. A. KILBURN (Waterford City), seconded in a few well chosen words and went so far as to venture the hope that women would not only receive the vote but also the right to membership of that House and all its privileges. (Laughter.) Mrs. C. E. PARKER then led off ?or the ladies. Sup- porters and opposers alike, she said, recognize that women are in earnest in asking for a vote, but I hold that this is only part of a larger question—the right to assert their individuality and claim to full human equality. In early times woman was treated merely as the adjenct of man, through long ages regarded AS private property, to be bought and sold, carried captive by brute force, and punished arbritarily, but in the evolution of the race woman has been gradually</p>
      </div>
