      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 11, 1908.</p>
        <p>rUDLIC V. E3REWER.</p>
        <p>LAST NIGHT'S DEMONSTRATION FOR THE BILL.</p>
        <p>Forged Tickets No Avail.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was last evening the scene of a great demonstration promoted by the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League in support of the Government Licensing Bill. The building was crowded to its -utmost capacity—in fact hundreds clamourad in vain for admission and an overflow meeting had to be arranged in Bold street Schools. How deeply public opinion has been aroused in favour of the measure was evident from the . remarkable enthusiasm which characterised the proceedings. No public question of late years has created anything like the same interest and the tactics resorted to by "the trade" have earned the condemnation of all right thinking people. Some opponents of the Bill in Warrington even vs ent so far as to issue a large number of forged tickets for last night's meeting and a warning promptly appeared on the hoardings, at the instance of the Temperance League, warning the holders of such tickets that they would not be admitted. Great precautions had, however, to be evercised to prevent what was obviously an attempt to wreck the meeting, a large staff of police being on duty, whilst the tickets were checked so thoroughly as to entirely defeat the disgraceful attempt. The League had been fortunate in securing such excellent speakers as the Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse)' Mr. Charles Roberts M.P., (Lincoln), the Rev. Paul Bull (community of the Resurrection) and Mr. William Madden. The leading features of the measure were thus clearly explained from the different standpoints and the audience was quickly made to see the hollowness of the cry of " Robbery and Confiscation." The fearless stand of the Bishop on this question has gained the admiration of all earnest reformers and this was eloquently expreesed by the extreme cordiality of the reception which was accorded him. His lord- ship spoke with all the fervour of deep conviction and in a way which impressed the audience with the unanswerable nature of the case for the Bill. Mr. Roberts. M.P., who is an authority on the time limit,aml monopoly value, dealt most ably with various aspects of the question, and the speeches by the Rev. Paul Bull, who came from the Parish Church after addressing a service for men, and Mr. William Madden were powerful and convincing. Before the meeting began the Band of Hope Union Choir gave some admirable selections. Mr. JAMES DODDS (president of the League) pre- sided and was supported on the platform by the Bishop of Liverpool, Mr. Charles Roberts, M.P., Rev. Paul Bull, Mr. William Madden. There was a splendid " platform," the Chairman and speakers being supported by a huge body of Noncon- formists, Church laymen, and people of all shades of political opinion. As the Chairman advanced to the table there was a storm of applause from all parts of the building, a sign of enthusiasm which was again and again renewed as well-known supporters of the temper- ance cause were recognized. Amongst those on the platform we noticed were the Revs. John Yonge, C. Harvey Cook, S. Pitt, T. Miller, P. \V. Pheysey M.A. G. Jackson (Litherland), E. E. Peters J. H. Banks A. Mitchell (Burtonwood), Alfred Garland (representing the Rev. T. Galland Hartley), R, E. Letheren, W. J. Roberts, E. J. Gawne, F. Vaughan, J. F. Matthews, J. Gunnell, H. Russell, Messrs. ,H. Roberts, J.P., C. E. Parker, J.P., W. Roberts, J.P., C. J. Holmes, J.P.,Alderman Pierpoint. J.P., Alder- man Tinnion, J.P., Councillor Stott, J.P., David Garnett, J. W. Hayward, J.P., John Maginn, J.P., E. Boreland (United Kingdom Alliance), W. H. Sherburn, F. W. Monks. J.P., Conn. Hawthorn, E. V. Walker, A. Hnlme, F. Gandy, G. Davey-Simith, J. Griffiths, E. Lloyd-Jones, G. Carruthers, E. England, J. T. Rowland, W. Isaac, W. E. Plinston, W. Hewitt, W. Wright, F Maginn, J. Howes, jun., A. Mounfield, G. J. Brown, S Fernley, J. Morton, P. O'Brien, A. M. Crowe, Dr.. Bennett, J. Upson, H. Wardman, W. Bibby, S. W. Jeffery, G. Whitfield, H. W. Guest, T. Ashley, T. , Toulmm,T. Gandy, A. Dunne, P. C. Garnett, Jenks, 1 Nightingale, Councillor Barton, Edward Wallington,l Perris, H. Rathbone, G. Weaver, Pearce Williams, Councillor Skelland, F. J. Norman J.P., (Runcorn), R. H. Posnett J.P. (Runcorn), J. G. Withinshaw, A. Boulton Bishop, G. A. Leach, H. Woolley, and others. Very many of these gentlemen were accompanied by their wives, and altogether the platform was a very imposing one. A letter of apology for non-attendance had been received from Mr. William Long who wrote: "I feel sure you will have a large and elithusiastic gathering to welcome the Bishop of Liverpool and that our people throughout the country will be firm in the determina- tion to regain the control of the licensing in the interests of morality and freedom." The Chairman announced the receipt of a telegram from the Rev. F. A. Screeton, of Hyde, formerly vicar of Christ Church, Latchford : "Very much regret impossible to come to your meeting. All success to it.' (Loud applause.) THE CHAIRMAN'S ADDRESS. The CHAIRMAN said this meeting had been convened by the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League, a body -which embraced abstainers and non-abstainers, men and women of almost every creed, and of every political ,opinion, in short all who desired to promote national sobriety. (Applause.) Had this been a purely party meeting he would not have been there, indeed be did not think he would have been invited. He was there to :Ova an unqualified support to the Government "Licensing Bill, because he believed it to be an honest Bill, a moderate Bill, and so far as the brewers and licensees were concerned, a generous Bill—(hear, hear) and certainly it was a necessary Bill; and deserving of the united and the loyal support of every lover of his country. (Applause.) There was shallow talk about 4‘ Robbery and Confiscation." He was not surprised. ." Suspicion haunts the guilty mind." (Applause.) He believed when the people had grasped the teal scope and infection of the Bill, they would rally to its sup- port ; but the evil was so gigantic that those who were striving to overcome it would require TIIE AID OF THE CHURCHES, the temperance societies, and every possible good influence that could be brought to bear. (Applause.) The fight would be a tremendous one—one in which the Government would require the help of all moral and spiritual forces. As Mr. Lloyd-George so well said on 'Thursday night. " There are great forces against us. The most powerful trade in the country, the trade,' with a committee-room in every street, and a hired agent in every street, windows placarded, huge funds, the funds of the widows and orphans, all used for political purposes without the slightest regard for the poor widow or the poor orphans : all these mighty iteluences have beiten British parties in the past, and they will beat British parties again unless the people rise and workout their own salvation." (Applause.) He would have keen glad to have dealt with the main question involved, that was as to whether or not the -nation should have restored to it its right to recall the monopoly which the Act of 1901 confiscated to the brewers, but he forbore, as they had on the platform a gentleman who was the acknowledged authority on the point at issue. (Applause.) He would only ask them to Orally this question, and he had no fear that the result would be that they would determine to leave no atone unturned•until the blessings which the Bill would insure were in their grasp. In the words of Babcock he asked them to " Be strong t We are not here to play, to dream, to drift; We have hard work to do and loads to lift. Shun not the struggle, face it, 'tis God's gift. "Be strong I Say not the days are evil (who's to blame?) And fold the arms and acrptiesca. (Oh shame !) Stand up, speak out, and bravely, in God's name. " Be strong ! It matters not how deep entrenched the wrong, How hard the battle goes, the day how long, Toil on, feint not, to-morrow comes the song ! " .(Loud applause). MR. E. J. GUEST MOVES THE RESOLUTION. Mr. E. J. GUEST proposed the following resolution:— That this meeting heartily welcomes the Licensing Bill now before Parliament as a substantial measure of temperance reform, reducing excessive drinking facilities and securing the people the ultimate control of the liquor traffic, and trusts that more stringent regulations will be made in regard to the drinking clubs, and further calls upon all citizens to unite in this national effort to diminish the gigantic evils produced by the present licensing system. He said he moved the resolution with pleasure because he was fully convinced that the Licensing Bill was a thoroughly just measure, (Applause.) It wAs just to the people. The reforms proposed to be carried out had been due to us for many years. The monopoly value in licences belonged to the country. (Hear, hear.) It was reasonable that the magistrates should have full control in granting or refusing licences. It was fitting that licensed houses should be closed on Sundays and election days. (Hear, hear.) They welcomed the restrictions put on the evil practice of sending children to public-houses. (Applause.) They were glad that clubs were to be put under closer super- vision and that the majority of the inhabitants of a new neighbourhood were to have the veto. (Applause.) It was only right that they should be able to say whether they would or would not have a licensed house</p>
        <p>in their midst. It was just, nay, even generous to the Trade. (Applause.) The Licensed Victuallers "do protest too much." The</p>
        <p>HOLLOWNESS OF THEIR CRY, that this measure was a violation of the Eighth Com- mandment, would appear more and more clearly as the nature of their claim was put side by side with the pro- visions of the Bill. He could quite understand that self interest should cause them to cry out. But their cry would have more weight if it were less vehement. (Hear, hear.) All the knowing ones in the Trade were well aware that they had no vested interest in licenses. If they did not know it before they knew it well enough after the Over-Darwen case was decided in 1882. Their own legal adviser, Mr. Thomas Nash, told them very distinctly on the sth September, 1883. " that, sub- ject to appeal, the Licensing Magistrates can refuse to renew the licence of any and every bolder of an on-licence." And he warned them in very emphatic language not to remain an instant in a state of false security." They knew very well that they had taken his advice, and had in the past 25 years formed 345 breweries and distilleries into limited companies. And now they asked the public to PITY THE WIDOWS AND PROFESSIONAL GENTLEMEN I who had invested their savings in insecure property. If these were not informed of this insecurity they were the dupes of the Trade and the Trade should compensate them for any loss they might sustain. (Applause.) The country realised, if the Trade did not,that the monopoly value foolishly given away had raised up a power in their midst which must be controlled. (Hear, hear.) The Trade had been enriched at the expense of the State. This Bill said that in 14 years from April 1.909 this anomaly should cease to exist. (Applause.) A child did not care to have a toy taken from it, although it was only lent for a time. A beggar in receipt of a gratuity felt defrauded if the gratuity was not continued. And the Trade cried out our craft is in danger" because it was no longer to be bolstered up at the expense of the country. (Applause.) He protested against this hollow cry of robbery. (Applause.) There was not a lady or gentle- man on that platform who would be there if they had a suspicion that they were supporting a violation of the 'eighth Commandment. Such a cry as that was utterly unfair. It was hitting beneath the belt, and would recoil on those who were making use of it. (Applause.) I This Licensing Bill instead of proposing to do an 'injustice was by granting a time limit of 14 years MINGLING MERCY WITH JUSTICE. In 1871 Dr. Bruce proposed a time limit of 10 years, which might have come into force if the Teniperance Party had been wise enough in their generation and had accepted his Bill. The Bill was reviving once more the spirit of social reform, which had been allowed to lie dormant of late. There never was so true a spirit of altruism existing in the country as there was to-day ; but it needed to be put into action. The licensed houses were so numerous that they could only be main- tained by excessive drinking. They were told that only one half per cent. of the inhabitants of this country were convicted of drunkenness. But this by no means represented all the excessive drinking that was constantly going on. There were multitudes of men and women drinking to excess who were wise enough to keep out of the Police Court. It was a foolish cry on the part of the Trade that if they lessened the number of licensed houses they would increase the number of unemployed and increase the misery of the people. For every man or woman thrown out of employment by this measure there would be many to whom new opportunities to work would be afforded. The</p>
        <p>LACK OF EMPLOYMENT in this country was largely due to the fact that money, physical and mental vigour were wasted by excessive drinking, and this meant that remunerative business was lost to the country. The Bill was bringing into line all social reformers of many varied opinions in regard to other subjects. ,1 (Applause.) He gave a very hearty welcome to the Bishop of Liverpool—(loud applause)—and assured him that as far as that platform was concerned t6ere were there no Nonconformists. They were all prepared to follow his lead on this question. He recognized in him a man who was made of such stuff as , martyrs were made of. (Applause.) Neither THREATS NOR PROMISES would turn him aside from the clear path of duty. (Applause.) He (Mr. Guest) was glad to think that the lead the Archbishop of Canterbury and other I Bishops were giving would smooth the way to an , amicable settlement of the Education question. (Applause.) The conscience of the Churches had long been alarmed at the spirit of controversy that existed amongst professing Christians. (Hear, hear.) Anythin4 that united them as the licensing question so largely did would, he felt sure, pave the way , to a cessation of the strife of tongues and 1 to an adjustment of their differences on equit- able lines. (Applause.) He moved the resolution with pleasure because :—(1) The Licensing Bill was just to the people, just and generous to the Trade ; (2) It was bringing into active operation the spirit of altruism that existed amongst us ; (3) It was giving us 1 to see that amid all our differences and controversies there was underlying a deep desire for unity and peace. t ( Applause.) Councillor JAMES BALL, speaking on behalf of the Labour party,said it gave him great pleasure to have the I privilege of seconding the resolution. The leaders of the labour organizations, he said, knew very well the terrible ravages which the insidious temptation of I drink brought about in the homes and lives of the I people, and the working-classes themselves knew how that temptation had helped materially to produce the unhappy conditions which obtained in our large centres of population. They therefore approached this Bill, not so much from the point of vies of the extreme temperance idea but from the point of view as to how it bore upon the general amelioration of the conditions of the people. They welcomed it because it was an honest attempt to deal with a most perplexing problem. (Cheers.) He did not believe the mere restriction of licences would have the far-reachinc, advantages they so much desired, but he looked upon the Bill not solely by itself but in connection with the efforts which the Government were making in the way of housing, land reform and social reform generally. It would be the duty of the Labour members to see that the fourteen years time limit was not extended, as it was quite ample. (Applause.) THE BISHOP'S SPEECH. The LORD BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL supported the resolution. He was received with tumultous cheering, the audience rising to their feet enmasse. He made no apology, he said, for standing there that night to sup- port this resolution. He was not there as a party (Hear,hear.) If this were a party measure, like the Chairman he would not be present at that meeting. (Cheers.) Nor was he there as an abstainer, ' though for 37 years he had been a total abstainer. (Cheers.) He was there as an English citizen and as an English Bishop to lift up his voice, feeble as it might be at a great crisis in the history of the country. (Cheers.) For thirty-five years as a curate, as a vicar, and as head of a Theological College, he had laboured for temperance reform. (Cheers.) lie would be craven hearted indeed if he did not adhere to the line he had so low, followed, and refused to discharge at such a time the duty of speaking for what he believed to be a just and useful Bill. (Cheers.) When he remembered Lord Rosebery, who often spoke with great weight, said that if the country did not control the trade the trade would control the country, when he remembered that one of the representatives of the trade publicly stated that " their trade was their politics " he felt it was time that every Englishman who loved his fatherland should do what he could,not to destroy the trade but to regulate it and keep it within bounds. (Loud applause.) Now, he was told that the Bill meant robbery, ruin to the orphan and the widow and that it did not make for temperance.</p>
        <p>IF IT MEANT ROBRF.RY he wished to know what it was they were stealing. (Hear, hear.) Was it the publican's house? They did not take a stone. Was it the publican's land ? They did not take an inch. Was it the publican's furniture? They did not take a stick. What they did in the Bill was this : they reclaimed for their country the com- plete control of the licences—(applause)—which a few years ago it weakly parted with. (Applause.) Since the year 1552 the State had always claimed com- plete control. (Hear, hear.) During the last three or four centuries that discretion was never doubted. The legislation of the reign of George IV., the judicial decision of the Over-Darwen case and of Sharpe v. Wakefield declared without doubt that the magistrates had the fullest discretion in the matter of licences ; that a licence was granted yearly, and for one year alone. The Act of 1904 reversed the whole tenour of English legislation. It practically gave a freehold in the licence to the brewer and the publican. (cheers.) No ! This Bill did net mean robbery, it meant the State claiming her own and taking it back. (Cheers). It did not mean I rain to the orphan and the widow. If there were any at this moment, who were at all likely to lose money I through brewery shares they could not withhold from them their deepest sympathy but, he would have them I remember four things. 'The Times" in March last reminded us that brewers' shares were not killed by the Licensing Bill but that they had been killed for some time by the wild-financing and over capitalisation by the brewers themselves. (Cheers). He would remind them in the second place that of the 360 brewery com- panies which now exist 345 of these had been launched since the Over-Darwen case which distinctly affirmed that a licence was granted for one year and was within the complete control of the licensing justices. (Cheers). If the advisers of the widows and orphans recom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Majority 2,000 to Two.</head>
        <p>Majority 2,000 to Two.</p>
        <p>mended them to invest their money in brewery shares under such circumstances it was their advisers who ought to be responsible for the mistake. (Cheers.) He would remind them that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TRADE -WOULD NOT CJM ETO AN END</head>
        <p>THE TRADE -WOULD NOT CJM ETO AN END</p>
        <p>as some people seemed to imagine, judging from their hysterical outcry, at the end of 14 years. (Applause.) He trusted there might be a diminution of the trade, but a man as capable as Sir Thomas Whittaker had told them again and again that at the utmost the trade would be reduced by only one third and considering its enormous volume at present there would be plenty left for the orphans and widows. (Cheers.) And on this point let them remember the other victims of intemper- ance. (Hear, hear.) Take into account all that innumerable army of men and women and little children whose lives had been blighted,whose characters had been ruined, whose homes had been desolated, through strong drink—(hear, hear)—and the tens of thousands of little children who to-day were hungry, ill-clad, diseased, as a consequence of strorv, drink. Take into account also the ratepayers who bad to pay an increased rate to sup- port the tens of thousands of victims of intemperance in our workhouses and in our asylums. (Cheers.) What he would like to see was THE TRADE MADE RESPONSIBLE</p>
        <p>Ifor the upkeep—(cheers)—of every lunatic, and every ' pauper, and every criminal whose crime and lunacy and pauperism could be traced to strong drink.(Cheers.)They were told this Bill wonld not make for temperance. Well, then, why in the world did the brewers oppose it? (Loud applause and cheers.) If the Bill would not diminish the sale of strong drink, why in the world did they not as patriots side with the Bill ? (Loud applause and cheers.) He admitted that the Bill could be strengthened. He trusted it would be strengthened. (Hear, hear.) He could wish that Grocers' Licences—(cheers)—which—and he spoke from personal knowledge—were doing untold damage to the character of the women of England, could be I abolished entirely. (Cheers). He wished with all his I heart that a clause could be introduced compelling . earlier closing on a Saturday evening. (Hear, hear). He wished that a clause was introduced in favour of pure and unadulterated drink. (Cheers). Why should the labourer in Scotland-road, Liverpool, have supplied to him drink which he had heard described as brim- stone and tire, while the more educated and richer neighbour of his in the centre of Liverpool, neat the Exchange. could obtain the pure article. (Cheers). IHe wished that a stronger clause could be introduced 1 for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>REGULATION OF CLUBS.</head>
        <p>REGULATION OF CLUBS.</p>
        <p>(Cheers) which unless they were regulated were likely to prove of untold mischief in this country and he spoke of the rich man's club as well as the poor man's. (Cheers.) They were sometimes told that the diminution of the public-houses did not mean the diminution of strong drink. He would advise them to read a short letter by Dr. Dawson Burns, who at the ge of SO, with intellect unclouded and natural forces 1 unabated was lighting in the vary van of temperance reform. (Cheers.) He showed it was a subject which could not be proved by statistics because the licenses in one town meant one thing, licenses in another town meant another thing, because the regulations were very stringent, and in other places somewhat lax, but from personal experience the unanimous verdict was that the reduction of public-houses did mean a diminution of drunkenness. (Cheers). To go no further than Liverpool, the reduction of public houses there had meant a striking diminution in drunkenness. (Applause.) If the arrests were on the increase it was due to the increased watchfulness and strictness of the police,and he for his part thanked God for that increased watchfulness. (Hear, hear.) He did not, as Councillor Ball had said, expect that that Licensing Bill, if it became an Act, would at once pro- duce a temperance millennium,but he was perfectly sure , that it would lay a</p>
        <p>FOUNDATION OF A NEW ERA in the social history of England. (Loud applause.) When the fourteen years had passed and the control of licences passed to the body of the people, then the nation would speak out with no uncertain voice— (applause)—and then many a reform for which at present we longed in vain would be ours at once. Of one thing he was certain—that when the victory had been won and the smoke lifted from the battlefield, when the voices of the combatants were hushed and the clash of arms_ was stilled, their children and their children's children would bless them for supporting that great measure which had helped to make the England in which they lived a merry England, and a sober England in deed and in truth. (Loud and prolonged applause.) The Bishop then left to address the overflow meeting. SPEECH BY MR. C. ROBERTS, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHARLES ROBERTS M.P., for Lincoln, author of the book" The Time Limit," who on rising to speak received an ovation, said he read in the" Warrington Guardian "an account of a meeting at which it was stated that in Lancashire the people were practically as one man dead against the Licensing Bill. (Laughter and Oh ! oh !) Where you in this meeting come from I don't know. (Laughter.) Anyhow I thank you very heartily for the warmth of the welcome you have given to me who am indeed a stranger. (Cheers.) Commend- ing the courageous action of the Bishop of Liverpool, the speaker alluded to the opposition which was organized at Liverpool against the Bill, and proceeded to say that when the liquor trade prevented temperance advocates from stating their ease they did a great service to the cause, even at the cost of some temporary inconvenience to the speakers. They thanked the Bishop for lighting for this Bill undeterred by menace and clamour, and he (Mr Roberts)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CONGRATULATED 'WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>CONGRATULATED 'WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>on behaving a g-OC.d deal better than Liverpool did on the previous evening. (Hear, hear and Laughter). He asked himself: Why is it that in England we have not won this fight? (Cheers). They had won the battle in the United States, in the British Colonies. in Canada and in New South Wales. (Cheers). If they gave "The Trade " in England the same treatment as was meted out to them in the United States and Canada they would be howling, and roaring deaance in a way which they were not doing even now, (Hear, hear). What was the force which had won this battle with the trade in the United States and the Colonies ? It was the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>VIVIOIsi OF TTIE CHURCHIS</head>
        <p>VIVIOIsi OF TTIE CHURCHIS</p>
        <p>(Loud Cheers). We had not attained that unity in this country but he trusted that without any sacrifice of principle all who really cared for right, to whatever denomination they belonged, might be united against the common evil drink —(Cheers.) The messure before the country was one worth fighting for and he trusted that all who cared for the promotion of temperance would give it their hearty support. (Cheers.) The provisions of the Bill even in reference to clubs were much stronger than people had realized. The good they were going to get out of the Bill would not be wrecked by the institution of clubs. Dealing with the reduction of licences Mr. Roberts asked what about Warrington? The breweries were going to be ruined—(laughter)—</p>
        <p>, - THE RELVOIR HOUNDS were going to mourn their master. (Laughter.) One third of Messrs. Peter Walker's men were going to be dismissed, they were told. He had worked it out and he hoped it would not be a disappointment to them, but compulsorily under the statutory obligation there would be nu reduction of licences in Warrington at all. ("Shame.") They were such a model community —(laughter)--that they would not get any compulsory reduction. The justices would have the power of having a little optional reduction if they liked, but there would be no reduction under the compulsory clauses. The decrease of licences would be of real value because it would weed out the least well-managed houses, set up a competition among the survivors not to hold the worst conducted houses, and mostly effect the people who were crowded together in the drink enslaved dis- tricts in the great cities, to whom it would come with. deliverance. Mr. Roberts denied that the Bill was the pure milk of unadulterated Socialism. If so, how was it that Mr. Victor Grayson opposed it, while the Arch- bishop of Canterbury supported it? True the Bill claimed to recover for the State the value which the State had created, and that was perfectly justifiable. (Applause.) The expiration of the time limit would, he believed, see the END OF THE TIED. HOUSE SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>He had great sympathy for the individual investor, but i there were fluctuations n every trade. The Govern- ment could not guarantee investments. He did not believe in the cases of hardship which the brewers alleged would take place under the Bill. (Applause.) Were the brewers going fo suffer very much ? Licensees were yearly tenants as much as the tenant of a house or farm, who was subject to six months notice. That was the law. The brewers admitted it. Why should the hands of the community be tied because it man expected something. (Cheers.) Dealing with THE TIME LIMIT</p>
        <p>the speaker showed that members of all parties were in favour of the time limit and in 1923 they were going to have the model system invented by Mr. Balfour. At that time the licenses would be subject to the will of the magistrates and the locality, and then would be left reasonable profit to the brewer and the tenant, but the monopoly value would revert to the community. (Applause). This was Mr. 13alfour's model plan for new licenses by his Act of 1901. He contended that obtain years sw macsoamppeines' and in that time the owners could in the compensation value, the death duty value and market price, at the same time obtaining a reason- able percentage of the capital invested in the licences. He defied the brewers to show that they were morally or legally entitled to a single penny beyond what</p>
        <p>Was</p>
        <p>the Government had granted.—Applause. The 1 brewers said there was to be no surrunder ; that ' they would not take the Bill in any shape or form. That raised the question as to whether they were to rule and dictate to England—applause—and whether this great nation was to be for ever subservient to a demoralizing trade. The country had stood their domination too long. Now was the time for the State to recov3r its own.—Applause.—This was a fight between money-bags and financial interests against men and women and tho moral issues involved. It was this fact that inspired them in this great cause and led them to battle for that brighter future for which they all craved.—Applause. THE DESERTION BY THE CLERGY..</p>
        <p>- - - - The CHAIRMAN observed that he took occasion to' invite the local clergy to support the Bishop at that meeting.—Hear, hear.—Without exception, one and all began to make excuse and they had no representative of the Warrington clergy upon the platform.—(Shame.) —But we have present a gentleman whom we are all proud of for his outspokenness and his interest in temperance reform, he referred to the Rev. A. M. Mitchell.—Cheers. _</p>
        <p>The REV. A. M. MITCHELL of Burtonwood who had a hearty reception said he would have felt himself guilty of a deadly sin if he had absented himself from that great and grand meeting—Cheers. He had nothing to say about his brethren in the town but he did say this, that he regretted they were on the platform a short time ago and he still more regretted that they were not on the platform that night—Cheers.— He rejoiced that this bill was likely to bring about unity in religion.—Hear, hear.—He was proud, for once, of the Church of England Temperance Society. He confessed he had not given support to that society for 12 or 11 years because he had thought the society weak-kneed and not so whole- hearted as it ought to be.—Applause-.—But the Society had redeemed its character and now stood before the the country as entitled to the support of every right- thinking man and woman.—Applause.—This crisis marked an epoch in the history of the great National Church. They had come to the parting of the ways. They had to say" Good-bye, sweetheart, good-bye."- -Laughter.—They had to say "GOOD-EYE MR. BREWER, go your own way and keep your money."—Cheers.—They could live without it. They could do better with jam— great laughter and cheers—Give Mr. Hartley a cheer— renewed cheering.—He, Mr. Mitchell, had worked in Warrington and he knew it was as beer ridden as it was a drunken and dirty town.—laughter—Let it be now known that Warrington had changed its views on the temperance question and was wholly on the side of the Licensing Bill.—applause. THE REV. PALI. BULL, who had a splendid recep- tion, said he was thankful to be,able to bear witness on the point that they ought to support the Government with every possible enthusiasm on that Bill, though he had no tie or political connection or any affection at all for the Government, with which he was altogether in disagreement—Laughter.—He hoped he might be taken as one who felt that the subject was far</p>
        <p>ABOVE ALL PARTY CONS'IDERXTIONS' —Applause—and that everybody who loved his home ' and country ought to stand side by side to get the Bill passed—Applause.—There was another reason that he ventured to mention—that many of them in the Church of England were so thankful to have an opportunity of working side by side with those from whom they wera separated on the education question—Applaule.—Thi s was as much the cause of the poor little children as the education question because they were working to shatter the power of the most terrible monopoly that had ever ridden upon the back of England—Applause— While he was anKions to do entire justice to everybody as far as possit le, he believed that for one widow and orphan who might possibly be injured by the fall of Brewery Shares which took place long before this government came into power—Applause.—therewere ii hundred widows 'and orphans who were so on account of the drink evil. As patriots and those who loved their dear country, they ought to work together with vehemence and endeavour to accomplish this deliverance which he believed was the greatest that Eugland could work for itself.—Applause. MA.DDEN, who had a cordial reception, said he did not know whether he was the only representative of his faith on the platform. If he were he was proud of it—applause—and proud of being on the same platform as the Bishop of Liverpool. He was proud of the ministers of religion on the platform, and hoped the time would come when the clergy of his own Church would join with them.—Applause. He hoped that the representatives of Ireland in the House of Commons would be on the side of temperance and sobriety.— Applause. He believed there was a feeling aroused in England that was strong enough to carry this Bill to a successful issue. He citne to support the Bill, and he hoped the people would say with the old reformers that they would have the Bill, the whole Bill, and nothing but the Bill.—Cheers. He would like to have seen a clause to say that no brewer should have a tied house, and that no tenant should have a licence except a bona fide tenant and that there should be no possibility of trying to defraud the justices with dummy agreements.—Applause—Mr. Balfour worked his compensation scheme for his brewery supporters and the publicans were beginning to see that the Bill was in their favour- they were beginn- ing to see they need no longer be the tied hound of the brewer, obliged to put his placards and his boards out side their door—Applause.—They were beginning to breathe the air of freedom, and to feel that were to be emancipated; that they were to haven free licence and be able to work for their wives and families and not for the brewer, liable to be kicked out at a week's notice, to go to the workhouse or elsewhere as circumstances compelled them—Applause. There was no more hearty supporter of the Bill than he was. He was not advocating the cause of any great monopoly or of brewers. No man on that platform had anything to make out of the Bill. ' Every man on that platform was holding up the standard of morality and citizenship.—Hear, hear.—They were present to defend the interests of the women and children, and the men of this land whose lives were in danger of the liquor traffic.—Cheers.—As to the opponents of the measure, the result of the beating of the big drum at Peckham had been to solidify the opposition and to rally every man worth his salt in the defence of this Bill.—Loud applause.— Re had no doubt that at the next election at Warrington there were many men who would put aside their polit- ical views to strike a blow in the cause of that great ' Bill—the greatest brought forward by any statesman for a century—Applause.—Let them contrast the sacrifices which the brewers professed they would have to make, if this Bill became law with the human suffering of I men, women, and children, the victims of insanity and I crime, the sufferings of orphans as the result of the drinking habits of their parents—Hear Hear.—Alluding to the argument that there would be a loss of revenue t if the bill passed, Mr. Madden urged that the 33 ' millions collected from the brewers was spent on gaols, workhouses, asylums and other institutions which 6 were rendered necessary by the drink evil—Cheers. ' If workingmen instead of spending their money on • drink devoted it to better purposes there would be no need to ask Parliament for money for old-age pensions. —Applause.—ln the meantime he asked them to join under the banner of this modern St. George—the Arch- bishop of Canterbury—who was leading the masses. the ; army of the people, to a death struggle with the dragon of drink—Cheers.— They were all invited to the con- test and the result of it would be that we should see a I free and, regenerated land, a sober empire,a sober nation with a free people—loud cheers—When we reached that happy time we should discover the angel of peace—the cornucopia shedding blessings far and wide over the heads ot a free, a brave, and an emancipated and sober nation.—Loud cheers. RESOLUTION PASSED.</p>
        <p>The resolution was then put to the meeting and enthusiastically carried with only two dissentients. VOTES OF THANKS. The REV. JOHN YONGE, introduced by the Chairman " the Grand Old Man of Warrington" moved a vote of thanks to the speakers and said the meeting indicated a resurrection of the temperance cause in Warrington. They rejoiced that the Bishop had headed their cause. Applause. The CRA.MAN said it was peculiarly gratifying to him to ask a gentleman to second the resolution who belonged o a family which for two or three generations had stood in the forefront of temperance work in Warringtoe, and he hoped he might be spared to lead and help them in the temperance cause in Warrington for some time to come. He referred to Mr. Henry Roberts—Applause. MR, H. RuuEaTs, who was greeted with cheers, then seconded the resolution. They all knew, he said, what had transpired recently in connection with the action of the Bishop of Liverpool, and what had occurred only the previous night had, if possible, endeared him more to them and made them think more and better of him than they did before.—Applause.—He, Mr. Roberts, had a great deal of sympathy with the Bishop. There was I more than one point of similarity between them laughter—and he would undertake to say that if they wanted an exhibition of pluck, determination, endurance, and confidence, he would back a little man against a big one any day—laughter and applause.—He came there from a sense of duty, not to express any opinion about the Bill, but simply to show them, his old fellow-workers in the temperance cause, that be sailed unier the old banner yet, and he hoped in the near future he might have other and better opportunities of serving them_applause—and of serving his town in a similar capacity to what he had done in years gone by.—Applause. The vote was heartily passed. The Bi.hop acknowledged the vote. THE CHAIRMAN'S THANKS. The services of the chairman were acknowledged on the proposition of the Bishop, seconded by Mr. E. .1. Guest. In reply,the chairman said the success of the meeting was the best thanks he could have. 1 The Bishop then closed the meeting by pronouncing the benediction.</p>
        <p>-- - THE OVERFLOW MEETING. There was a large gathering at the overflow meeting in Sold-itreet Wesleyan Schoolroom. The Rev. C. Harvey Cook presided, and addresses were delivered by the Bishop of L ' iverpool,. Mr. Charles Roberts M P Rev. Paul Bull, Rev. T. Miller and Coun. Stott. There were nuoierous interruptions here, but the resolution carriad by an oVerwhelming majority. -</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds never fails 1/liv 2/9.—Advt,</p>
        <p>Former Warrington Clergyman's Views. Speaking at a Hyde demonstration in support of the Bill on Tuesday night, the Rev. F. A. Screeton, vicar of St. Thomas's, and formerly Vicar of Carist Church, Latch- ford, said brewers in the past had not shown much con- sideration for widows ad orphans. "I have seen widows and orphans who have been made such by the brewers," he remarked.</p>
        <p>It was said that brewers might withdraw their church subscriptions if the clergy did not oppose the Bill. It was a question whether many of the clergy would care to take such subscriptions when they knew possibly of the misery that had been suffered in order for the money to be made. If his feelings were those of the rest of the clergy a threat of that kind would only stiffen their backs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Rev. T. Miller's Yiews.</head>
        <p>Rev. T. Miller's Yiews.</p>
        <p>At a popular concert at Bold-street School, the I Nev. T. Miller dealt with the Government Licensing Bill. He, first of all, contradicted a statement common in certain circles that Wesleyan Ministers were share- holders in breweries. " There is not a Wesleyan Minister who holds shares or is directly interested in the Liquor Traffic. Our hands are clean." The speaker limited himself to the Time Limit and Local Option. The speakers at the brewers' demonstration did not mention that the reductions in licences were upon a scale lower than that suggested by the Peel Commission. After a reasonable provision had been established, licences could only be refused when a majority of those living in the immediate neighbour- hood were willing that the house should be closed. Surely this was to trust the people. But the brewers say "Temperance fanatics can- not trust the people." /n respect to new licences the decision rested with the people in the dis- trict concerned and not with the Licensing Magistrates who might be teetotal advocates. Why are the Brewers afraid ? Simply because they cannot trust the paople: The Government Bill was moderate, clear, and per- fectly just. It was not the working man the Brewers cared for ; it was interest and dividends. With them the rights of property were more sacred than the rights of man or God. The saddest feature as far as Warring- ton was concerned was that the Drink Traffic was so great a supporter of the Church that the clergy were found on the aide of a demoralising trade and against true social reform. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Mr. Seddon, LP, and the ticensed Victuallers.</head>
        <p>Mr. Seddon, LP, and the ticensed Victuallers.</p>
        <p>THE EVILS OF THE TIED-HOUSE SYSTEL In reply to the Secretary of the EArlestown Licensed Victuallers' Association, who forwarded a resolution against the Licensing Bill passed by his Association, Mr. Seddon, M.P., replied as follows : House of Commons, 28th March, 1908. Dear Sir,—l regret delay in answer to yoar letter. In addition to ill-health I have had many engagements, and my correspondence is a little in arrear. I am sorry to be unable to agree with your wholesale condemnation of the Licensing Bill. I am always willing to consider the views of my constituents, and 1 observe that your resolution states that the Bill " has not the justification of promoting temperance." I am interested to learn that your Association is of opinion that the promotion of temperance would be a justifica- tion of the Bill, and I should be glad if you would inform me how the Bill can be amended so as to pro- mote the cause of temperance. I presume you agree that the Bill in its operation will not have the effect of closing, during the 14 years' time-limit, a single well-conducted house in Earlestown, Newton, Ashton, Haydock, Golborne, or any of the country districts in the Newton Division. On the other hand I think you will agree from sad experience that the system of Tied Houses is one that presses heavily on your Association, by which you are robbed of the liberty of buying liquor where you desire,and are forced to pay 15s. or so more a barrel for beer than a house which is not tied to a brewery. Further, you will agree that the system does not consult the public con- venience, and the right of the citizen to buy any quality of liquor that he may desire is defeated ; although the licence is granted for the express purpose of meeting the public convenience. If you will kindly inform me how I can help you to remedy these and other defects of the present system, with the object of promoting the cause of temperance, I shall be pleased to give your suggestions my careful consideration.</p>
        <p>As this is a matter of public interest I am sending a copy of this correspondence to the Press.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>J. A. SEDDON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>_ To Mi. Harry Owen, Earlestown Licensed Victuallers' Association.</head>
        <p>_ To Mi. Harry Owen, Earlestown Licensed Victuallers' Association.</p>
        <p>The Bill and its Critics. [To THE .EDITOR.]. .</p>
        <p>Sir,—The campaign of rhodomonta'de and cant against the Government Licensing Bill still pursues its course ; every interest and every source by which pressure can be brought to scotch the measure is being roused to fever heat. There is no doubt that if the present ineaaurefails it will only result in a future Government being forced by public opinion to bring forward and carry through a far stronger measure than the present one, and which the brewing interest will be unable to resist. lam surprised at the opposition spirit shown towards this measure as if it were immediately going to become law in its present shape, when it is a well known fact that the first reading of a measure commits the country to nothing inparticular,and may be consider- ably modified and varied in progress through Parliament. Mr. Asquith has simply raised the temperance banner to which we heartily rally in the hope of securiir, some improvement in the habits of our country an that those habits need improving is clear from the fact that while the national expenditure of our country for the last year amounted to £151,575,000 it cost according to the latest returns £167,016,200 to quench the national thirst. In other words the national thirst cost £15,441,200 more than the whole army and navy and the whole of the civil government of the country put together. There is something radically wrong here. Time was when I had some respect for the Squire of Walton, but that has gone by and I feel amazed at the effrontery, tyranny and impertinence of the brewing trade. I was never more amazed than when I read the report of the Parr Hall meeting of the 26th of March and realized the un-English methods pursued to manufac- ture opposition to the Bill. Every employe of the trade that could be spared, from the Barrister at Brewster Sessions down to the humble barman or barrel washer, was there all the customers they could prevail upon were there to cheer anything and every- thing. Most sorrowful of all, the moralists, those who are supposed to teach men the way they ought to go, the clergy,had to toe the line shoulder to shoulder with the builders up of this iniquitous trade. Verily I hope they felt comfortable in their surroundings and that none of them will have to complain of empty churches. Surely, Sir, this is an un-English proceeding and I may note here that one of your contemporaries discribes the Bishop of Liverpool as a renegade. Who are the renegades? Is it the good Bishop who prefers to risk temporal loss, or is it the local clergy who prefer the loaves and fishes rather than uphold the moral and religious teaching that they inculcated in the past? Apologising for the length of this letter.—l remain yours etc., PHIL SQUO D. Mr. W. P. Rylands and Monopoly Value.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR.] Sir,—Mr. W. P. Rylands in a recent speech on tbe Licensing Bill in Fairfield Ward said " As to the monopoly value, that arose from three things : first, of course, from the energy and business capacity of the man who lan the house; secondly, from the capital invested ; and thirdly, it was partly owing to the licence from the State. Without any one of those there would be no value at all, and it was therefore wrong that the State should step in and take the whole." lam always interested in reading what Mr. Rylands says because whether we agree with him or not he reasons a matter out and does not content him- self with unsupported statements and does not stoop to cheap vituperation. I cannot, however, follow him in the above. Tnis value, in as far as it is a monopoly, cannot arise from the energy or capacity of the man who runs the house or from his capital. Energy, capacity, and capital may employ and get as much as possible out of a monopoly but they in no sense create it. The monopoly value of a licence arises from the restrictive policy employed by the State and from nothing else. Lord Grey when 136 secured the licence for the village of Broomhill used neither energy, capacity, nor capital. When the Brewing Compauy offered him £lO,OOO fdr the licence there was not a brick upon the ground, the value was created entirely by the State declining to give anyone except Lord Grey right to sell beer in that village. There was a widow selling refreshments and temperance drinks in a neighbouring town; some of her friends suggested she should apply for a licence. She did so and the magistrates considered the licence might be an advantage to the district and granted it. As SOOn as the licence was given a Brewing Company came to this widow and induced her to sell the shop with the licence for £l,OOO. In this case as in the other the value arose entirely from the State monopoly and not in any sense from the energy, capacity, or capital of the party who secured the licence. The energy and capacity in this connection bears fruit at once, its reward is realized immediately, to use a commercial expression, it is a fair charge to write against revenue ; and as far as the capital is concerned in beer in stock, fixtures, Ltc., this is comparatively small and can readily be realized. If Mr. Rylands will give the matter a moment's thought he will see that the third reason he gives is the paramount factor in giving the value, in comparison with which the other factors he mentions are insignificant. The point is an important one, it goes to the root of the " Robbery and Confiscation " cry, If those who with monotonous reiteration repeat this cry would help to reason the question out,we should more readily come toa conclusion as to the justice, or otherwise, of the principles upon which the Bill is based. But let me assure the trade, and their advocates, that they will never convince the British electorate by merely shout- ing "Confiscation and Robbery," nor by a periodical</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="5" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
