      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>display of material resources. Doubtless for a time this clamour and display gained a certain support, but the time for reflection has come, and the electors are examining the ground upon which the claims of the trade and the State are based. The British people are at heart reasonable and just and will give every man his own.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, April 6th, 1908. Resolution at Wycliffe Church. The following resolution was passed at a meeting of Wycliffe Church, on .Sunday evening: "That this assembly of members and communicants of Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington, heartily rejoices in the Licensing Bill now submitted to Parliament by his Majesty's Ministers. It believes the Bill to be loudly called for by the needs of the nation, to be just in principle,and calculated to be of the greatest service to the whole community. It trusts that while every care will be taken to safeguard the legitimate interests of all persons concerned in the trade which it properly seeks to control, the Government will do everything in its power to pass the Bill into law." The resolution was signed on behalf the meeting, by the Rev. J. Yonge minister of the church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Meeting at Padgatb.</head>
        <p>Meeting at Padgatb.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday afternoon a meeting. to explain and discuss the Licensing Bill was held in the Wesleyan Church, Padgate. There was a large attendance. The Rev. W. Mellor presided. The rev, gentleman explained the main clauses of the Bill and held the opinion that the effect of it would be if passed into Law, of strengthening and improving the moral and religious life of the nation. Dr. Bennett then spoke on the Bill and the Drink Question from the medical standpoint and stated that owing to the vicious state of drinking in the country, many children from birth were hereditarily handicapped, and the nation consequently suffered. Mr. E. England dealt with the subject from the citizen's point of view, and stated that from whatever side the drink question was examined the nation was all the poorer, and demonstrated it in figures. Mr. W. E. Plinston spoke from the employer's standpoint, and Mr. Jarman from the working man's standpoint, and gave his experience of a few years residence in Burton-on- Trent. The addresses were listened to with marked attention by the audience, the speakers evidently being well up in the Bill and the particular parts to which each of them directed his attention. At the close of the meeting a great number of signatures were obtained to a petition supporting the Bill. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NOBLE GIFT.</head>
        <p>A NOBLE GIFT.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool and Mr. W. P. Hartley.</p>
        <p>The BishoP of Liverpool has received the following letter from Mr. W. P. Hartley, with a cheque for £5OO "My clear Lord Bishop,—l regret to notice that your action in support of the Licensing Bill is likely to cause some falling off in subscriptions to the diocesan funds.</p>
        <p>" I deeply sympathise with your action, and as a life- long Nonconformist I desire at this time to show my appreciation by enclosing a cheque for £5OO, to be used by your lordship for such diocesan funds as may suffer from a lack of support. —I am, my Lord Bishop, y6urs truly, W. P. HARTLEY. "The Lord Bishop of Liverpool."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FORTHCOMING MEETING AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>FORTHCOMING MEETING AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening next a meeting in support of the Bill will be held in the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Schools. Mr. F. W. Monks will preside and the speakers will be Mrs. Mahood, of Liscard, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. C. E. Parker, ,and Rev. E. J. Gawne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Warrington Musical Festival.</head>
        <p>Warrington Musical Festival.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me a small space in your columns to draw attention to the first Warrington Musical Festival to be held in the Parr Hall on Satur- day, May 23rd, 1903. The festival, which is to take the place of the Warrington Eisteddfod, so successfully managed by the Apollo Male Voice Choir, is on a broad basis, such as the Liverpool, Blackpool, Morecambe, Lancaster and Preston Festivals and should need no special appeal, but the Committee feel that the respon- sibility is great, and seeing that the entries will be numerous especially in the Chief Choral Competition for the prize value £l2 and a shield value £3O, the Committee earnestly appeal to every section of the people of Warrington for help to make this festival a great success financially. Donations will be thankfully received and acknow- ledged by Mr. Frank Hickman, hon. treasurer, Winmar- leigh-street, Warrington.—On behalf of the Committee, I am, yours truly, LLOYD JONES (Chairman). CURRENT POLITICS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Letter From Mr. A. IL Crosfield, M.P.</head>
        <p>Letter From Mr. A. IL Crosfield, M.P.</p>
        <p>Sir, —Parliament has been making history in more ways than one, and we have had many deeply interesting and important debates recently, so that bearing in mind your recent communication, perhaps a word or two on current events at this moment will not be out of place. To begin with, we have the prospect of A BRILLIANT BUDGET</p>
        <p>in which Mr. Asquith will have to dispose of a hand- some surplus. May I just summarise the position and the figures to-day, so far as I am able at present to do so?</p>
        <p>In the first place the revenue is about three and a half millions more than he had anticipated. In the second_ place, we have saved on expenditure in the last financial year more than a million sterling. In the third, place, we last year sat aside a million sterling for extra repayment of debt, so that the surplus will really amount to more than five millions ; and the question arises what will be done with this very handsome and substantial balance. We have of course every year both the New Sinking Fund, and the Old Sinking Fund, as they are called. Out of the New Sinking Fund we shall pay about ten millions of debt. The old Sinking Fund will consist of Mr. Asquith's realised surplus of more than five millions, so that this year we shall in all repay about £15,000,000 of our predecessors' debts. This huge sum represents a greater provision than has ever before been made in a budget for repayment of debt; and as the national debt stands at a discount,--so that as your readers are well aware £lOO sterling will pur- chase considerably more than £lOO in scrip,—so, the redemption of debt for this year amounts I believe in round numbers to about .£11,500,000 sterling. The strength and soundness of Mr. Asauith's finance is, I think, certain to appeal very strongly to men of busi- ness in all our great industrial constituencies, and it is no wonder that our national revenue, under a Free Trade system, is the envy of Chancellors in foreign Protectionist countries. Speaking of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PROTECTION</head>
        <p>PROTECTION</p>
        <p>we had a few nights since a highly significant debate on the very live issue which has yet to be fought oat in the future—this great controversy between Free Trade and Protection. Mr. Mond opened the debate in a speech of first rate ability. At the close we had a long speech from the Leader of the Opposition, which was one of the most significant, if not the most significant, which he has ever delivered on the subject. So far as it is possible to draw any certain deduction from that speech, it seemed clear to me and to everyone on our side who listened to Mr. Balfour that he definitely committed himself both to a wide extension of taxation on various commodities, and to a system of Preference to the Colonies, which, as Mr. Chamberlain has plainly told us, can only be carried out by taxing food. Mr. Balfour has shown himself in debate after debate marvellously dexterous in the art of skating lightly over thin ice—but that night one seemed to hear it cracking. The Confederates are pressing him to take the Protectionist plunge, and i even if he shivers a little at the prospect it s now absolutely plain that with the forces at work in the Conservative Party we shall, at the very next election, have to fight on the issue of Protection—as on one or two other issues as well—for the rights of citizens as against excessive privilege and monopoly, and in short for that financial system which is the basis alike of Liberal policy and of Lancashire's prosperity. For the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CONFEDERATES_</head>
        <p>CONFEDERATES_</p>
        <p>are in no way mincing matters. Their tactics and the kind of statements and arguments they make use of in the constituencies in support of full-blooded, unblushing Protection we are all familiar with after recent elections: what is perhaps not so well under- stood in the constituencies is the pressure that is being put on individuals in the Party by the group of Con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>federates. The attack on Lord Hugh Cecil, one of the most brilliant men the Conservative party ever had, naturally made some stir, and now it seems that Lord Robert Cecil has narrowly escaped the same fate ; while a deliberate effort will be made by the Tories to turn out Mr. Stewart Bowles. Mr. Bowles, like his father, is a man of very considerable Parlia- mentary ability, but he also is to be driven out of Parliamentary life because of his fidelity to Free Trade; and I hear that the fact of his being a Free Trader is the reason why the Hon. NV. F. D. Smith— son of the late Hr. W. H. Smith, who was leader of the Tory party in the House of Commons, and Member for the Strand—why he will not stand again for Parliament. Just a:: Mr. Balfour lost the support of so many of the wiser heads in his Cabinet—of the men of statesmanship and experience who would not follow Mr. Chamberlain's crude and reckless policy— so now, among the younger generation, the Confeder- ates are engaged on the profitable task of driving out of the Conservative party some of its very ablest members. I for one hope that when the time comea their fidelity to sound fiscal principles will not bet forgotten by our side. I don't doubt that great interest is taken in War- sington in the latest development of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EDUCATION CONTROVERSY.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION CONTROVERSY.</p>
        <p>Everyone will remember the great efforts that were made to bring about a peaceful settlement in December 1000. Mr. Birrell and Lord Crewe worked for give and take and compromise, as far as compromise in the name of commonsense and toleration could possibly be carried. Some of us backed them up with hearty, goodwill and ultimately in the House of Commons Protestants and Catholics joined together to give Mr. Birrell a great majority in the Lobby. Mr. Balfour is a great tactician, and a master of political strategy. But even from that Foint of view alone I could not help feeling then and since, that looking to the future he made, even from his own point of view, a great; mistake in inducing the Lords to wreck the Bill, and therefore waste all the time and labour spent on through months in the House of Commons. Well, at that time there was a shrewd suspicion that the Archbishop of Canterbury was on our side. I only mention that for what it is worth, the important; point being that to-day we know he is with us in striving to bring about a peaceful 'settlement. In the House of Lords last Monday he gave his hearty support to the attempt which the Bishop of St. Asaph. is making to settle the controversy. We shall no doubt: be told that the Bill which the Bishop has brought; forward does not contain any provision for Roman Catholics, so I think it better to say at once that if this Bill, or anything like it, is passed, we shall be certain to add to it a generous clause for contracting out the Roman Catholic schools. The question is therefore not one which really need alarm Roman Catholics at all. As far as one can judge here, my own view is that public opinion strongly favours this endeavour to put an end to this protracted denomina- tional squabble, and I hope in the very near future we shall be able to get at a concordat. Perhaps the best way will be for the Leaders of the different churches; to meet one another at a round table conference, and there discuss their difficulties.</p>
        <p>Meantime I venture to suggest that events have rather strikingly justified Liberals in the course they have taken in regard to Education, for as aforesaid in 1006 the House of Lords considered it right to prevent a settlement. Mr. •Balfour apparently thought it right and expedient that the controversy should continue, and yet, less than a year and a half after- wards,we have the Archbishop of Canterbury himself coming forward to renew the attempt so bravely made by Mr. Birrell to bring about a peaceful settlement. Can one imagine a more complete vindication of the whole spirit and tone of Liberal policy in reference to education? I will go a little further and say that in this, as in not a few other questions, it will be proved that in spite of all the clamour raised against us, Liberals do to-day really represent the average view of the average and reasonable man. Of course the action of the Primate can hardly be disassociated fromlhis STRONG APPEAL ON BEHALF OF THE LICENSING BILL. IHe sees of course that if the churches are to work together in the direction of genuine social reform,they must hot at the same time be squabbling over what shall ho taught in the schools. I will not prolong this letter by entering into a discussion of the Licensing Bill. I hope with your permission to have another opportunity of discussing that question and the great controversy that is raging round it a little later on. At the present moment may I just in conclusion venture to refer to the speech which I heard Mr. Asquith deliver at the dinner of the City ILiberal Club on Wednesday evening. With the burden of the Budget, of the Licensing Bill, and, at the present time, the Leadership of the Party all on his shoulders at once, he yet found time to deliver a truly admirable speech at that gathering. His open- ing sentences—perfect in tone and taste - expressed what was in the mind and heart of everyone, and then towards the end of his speech, in his reference to the controversy over the Licensing Bill, I am sure that yours readers will entirely agree with his fair and just view of it as well as with his clearly expressed determination, not to be deterred from a sound policy by any of the threats, exaggeration, and misreprentation which no doubt have for a time mislead but only temporarily mislead some people in the country.—Yours, etc.,</p>
        <p>A. 1.1: CRoSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>London, April 3rd, 1908. THE EASTER HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>London, April 3rd, 1908. THE EASTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Many people will now be discussing the question of where to go for the Easter holidays and so admirable are the arrangements by the railway companies that they should have little difficulty in making up their minds. The programme of the L. and N.-W.R. is most attractive, and should meet the demands of all, who are desirous of visiting friends, or who may be on pleasure bent. The list covers many parts of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales, and to some of the most prominent of these we would draw attention. To those intending to visit Ireland, it will be well known, that the North Western Company's route to the Emerald Isle, is the most popular and expeditious, the journey being made by a short sea route via Holyhead. To Greenore, and the North of Ireland, many cheap trips and circular tours have been fixed, and Greenore it may be said, is a convenient centre for golf and for excursions on Carlingford Lough, and through the Mourne mountain district, and other points. Through excursions to London are announced and there are also excursions to York, Scarborough, Newcastle and North-Eastern District, April 16th, to West of England by the direct and popular route via the Severn Tunnel, South and Central Wales, South Coast watering places and the Eastern Counties. Excursions to Scotland, all parts, on April 16th. The local tripper is also well catered for, and many special trips are arranged to Blackpool, North Wales Line, Morecambe, Windermere, Manchester, Liverpool, New Brighton, &amp;c. The short date week end and long date tickets to many places will be issued on Thursday' April lfith, Friday April 17th (where train service permits) and also Saturday. The London and North-Western and Great Western Railways have arranged excursions to Halton,Frodsham, Helsby and Chester on Good Friday and Easter Monday In connection with the Great Central Railway. Messrs. Dean and Dawson announce excursions on Thursday to London (Marylebone), Lincoln, Cam- bridge, Eastern Counties, North-East coast resorts, &amp;c.; on Good Friday to Sheffield, Gainsburough, Brigg, Grimsby Docks, Hull and Cleethorpes ; on Saturday to ' London, Eastern Counties, Nottingham, Leicester, &amp;c. ; Easter Monday to London, Nottingham, Sheffield, Worksop, Lincoln,Brigg,Grimsby Docks and Cleethorpes Those desirous of spending a few days in Devonshire or Cornwall are admirably catered for by the Great Westernßailway Company. FRODSEIAIII PLEASURE GROUNDS. The Mersey View Pleasure Grounds, Overton Hills, Frodsham, will open for the season as usual on Good Friday and Easter Monday. Crosfield's Prize Band will play for dancing each day, commencing at 1 o'clock and there will be numerous other attractions. Marriages. PALMER —HARRISON—ApriI 4th, at the Parish Church,. Warrington, by the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, Edward Balmer, to Eva Harrison. IN MEMORIAM. IN FAITHFUL REMEMBRANCE of my beloved Husband, Robert Garnett, Hall Nook, Peuketh, who died on April 9th, 1903. MARY GARNETT.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by ZOON WALKEN &amp; CO., at The Examiner General Printing Worksßerasey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road WIDNES 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, Market Street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street _RuNcoitai API• 11th, 1908.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
