        <p>purpose of preventing it. We never hear of beadles perambulating churches on the lookout for offenders nowadays, although possibly in remote parts the custom of waking sleepers during service still prevails in some of the old country churches. "High "Churches.—There are two or three senses in which a church may be " high," ecclesiastically, structurally, or physiograpthically—and sometimes this leads to confusion. A visitor in Kent was once making enquiries about the old parish church of Sta,plehurst and asked a local farmer if it was high. "About eighty feet," he replied.i One of the highest churches physio- graphically s St. Michael's, Dartmoor, for the repair of which funds are needed. It was built a century ago, by i the French and American prisoners of war, and s nearly 1,450 feet above sea level. There is only one "higher' church in the British Isles, the parish church of Neut- head, in Cumberland,at an elevation of 1,500 feet. MADGE.</p>
        <p>MATRIMONIA MISERIES. The Husband Rheumatic; The Wife's Health broken by Chills. Both made well by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. In the home where husband and wife is ill, unhappiness reigns; when both are afflicted the misery is complete. Whatever they may have suffered in the past Mr. and Mrs. SNEE, of 7, Union Street, Burnley, are to-day a healthy, happy young couple, and for this ideal state they bless the day when Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cured them.</p>
        <p>PENKETH. - WESLEYAN CLIURCH.—A highly successful concert in aid of the organ fund of this church was held in the Wesleyan schoolroon on Friday evening, Mr. F. Johnson in the chair. Songs were rendered by the choir, Miss Emily Taylor Miss Lilian Davey, and Mr. W. H. Ireland ; Master Ernest Millington gave a couple of violin solos; a duet was rendered by Mr. W. H. Ireland and Mr. H. Plumpton, and a trio by Misses Johnson, Ireland, and Aspey, whilst humorous Rents were given by Mr. J. N. Barker. Mr. G. Weaver discharged the duties of accompanist. • SLATER'S FATAL FALL.—An inquest was conducted by Mr. Brighouse,at Ws.rrington;on Tuesday, on the body of William Rigby, aged 55, a slater, who resided at Bank House Cottages, Penketh. He was employed by Captain Michael Hughes to repair a barn roof at Mr. Ditchfield's farm, and, according to Thomas Gallimore, a fellow workman, he was on the roof at a quarter to eight on Tuesday morning, March 28th, nailing down Some stepping boards. One of the boards bounced off, causing Rigby to slide down the roof and fall to the ground. He was attended by Dr. Murray and then removed to the Warrington Infirmary where he died on Saturday. Dr. Bate, agent for Captain Hughes, said Rigby was an experienced man and had worked on the roof before. He expressed Captain Hughes's sympathy with the deceased's relatives. A verdict of "Accidental death " *as returned. Soclat,—On Saturday evening a social and tea party was held in the Wesleyan day schools for students and friends Of the evening classes. The room had been most tastefully decorated and presented a very pretty appear- ance with its daintily set-out tables. Ile catering was in the capable hands of Mrs. Watson who had prepared a high-class repast which was done full justice to by the 60 who sat down. The gathering included the members of the commercial classes, sewing class and friends. After tea short addresses were given by Dr. Murray who explained the many advantages gained by those attend- ing evening classes, and Mr. Watson, the teacher, who said the classes hsd been well attended, and good work had been done. The remaining part of the evening was spent in games vocal and instrumental music. Solos were given by INliss Balshaw, Miss Atherton acting as pianist. Many selections were given on the Zonophone which had been kindly lent by Mr. W. H. Baker. A most enjoyable evening was brought to a close about eleven o'clock after the usual votes of thanks were given.</p>
