        <p>----- - LOCAL NEWS. Coming Events. At Wycliffe Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening the choir will sing "The Crucifixion" (by Dr. Stainer). The Sunday school anniversary in connection with Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church will be celebrated to-morrow (Sunday), when Mr. W. R. Reed, Hartley College, Manchester, will preach. On Monday evening a public meeting will be held. An open meeting of the P.S.A. Society will be held on Sunday afternoon The Rev. C. Harvey Cook will speak. ' Special services in connection with the foreign missionary anniversary at Bold-street Wesleyan Church are announced for to-morrow (Sunday) and Good Friday. The .annual meeting will be held on Good Friday evening. In aid of the Infirmary and District Nursing Association performances of the charming comic opera "The Emerald Isle "will be given in the Parr Hall by the Crosfield Amateur Operatic Society on Thurs- day, Friday and Saturday April 30th, May Ist and 2nd. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER TAB CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRIL—Advt. Temporary Authority. At the Borough Police Court on Monday temporary authority was granted Mr. Harry Wyatt to sell at the Ring o' Bells, Church-street, in place of Mr. Peers Brimelow. Mr. Jenkins made the application. On the application of Mr. Browne, Joseph Hatton was granted temporary authority to sell at the Mersey Inn, in lieu of Mrs. Butterworth. TRY GILLETTE SAFETY RAZORS for comfort in shaving.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Death of an Asylum Medical Officer. The death occurred on Monday of Dr. Fred. J. Macphail, who for several years had held the position of assistant medical officer at Winwick asylum. Dr. Macphail was the eldest on of the Rev. Dr. Macphail, until recently minister of Canning-street Presbyterian Church, Liverpool. The deceased gentleman, prior to taking up his position at Winvvick Asylum, was one of the best-known Rugby footballers in the district, being a prominent member of the Liverpool Old Boys Club, and for several seasons be distinguished him- eslf in the forward ranks of the Lancashire County I team, when he gained much local fame.</p>
